\id REV Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Apocalypse
\toc1 The Apocalypse of John the Apostle.
\toc2 Apocalypse
\toc3 REV
\mt1 The Apocalypse of John the Apostle.
\is THE ARGUMENT OF THE APOCALYPSE OF S. JOHN.
\ip That which the old Testament foretold of Christ himself, the Apostles could report the fulfilling thereof in the new Testament, by way of an history, even from his Conception to his Glorification. But of his Church, they could not do the like: because in their time it did but begin: being to continue long after them, even to the end of the world, and then at length to be glorified, as Christ her Spouse already is. Hereupon God would have S. Luke to report in the Acts of the Apostles, the story of the Church's beginning, and for the rest of it to the end, (that we might receive this benefit also by the Apostle's hands) he would S. John to tell us of it in this book by way of a prophecy. Of which book S. Hierome saith: \it The Apocalypse of S. John hath as many sacraments or mysteries,\it* \f - \ft \+it Hier. ad Paulin.\+it*\f* \it as words.\it* Yea more than that, \it In every word there are hid manifold and sundry senses.\it* Therefore it is very little that can here be noted, in respect. Yet to give the good Catholic (whose comfort is here) some little help, the book may be divided into five parts.
\ip The first (after the prooem) containeth seven Epistles from Christ now in glory, to seven \f - \ft \+it 1. part.\+it*\f* \f - \ft \+it Ca. 1. 2. 3.\+it*\f* Churches of Asia, or (for, these he maketh all one) to the seven Bishops of those Churches: meaning not to those only, but to all his Churches & Bishops throughout the world: saying therefore in every one of them, to all in general: \it He that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith to the Churches.\it* As also in every one he exhorteth us to fight manfully (in this spiritual warfare of ours against sin) for the victory, and in every one accordingly promiseth us a reward in heaven. But before this, in the beginning of every one, he partly commendeth, partly reprehendeth, and exhorteth to penance. Where this is much to be noted, and feared, that among so many, he reproveth somewhat in all, save only in two, which are the second & the sixth. In the beginning also of every one, he taketh some piece out of the apparition going before, to frame thereof his style agreeably to the matter of each Epistle.
\ip After this admonition to Pastors and their flocks: the second part followeth, wherein the \f - \ft \+it 2.\+it*\f* \f - \ft \+it Ca. 4. to the 8.\+it*\f* Church and whole course thereof from the beginning to the end, is expressed in the opening of a book in God's hand, and the seven seals thereof, by Christ. For the which, he seeth praise sung now in heaven and earth, not only to the Godhead, as before, but also (after a new manner) to Christ according to his Manhood. And here, when he is come to the opening of the last seal, signifying Domesday, he letteth that matter alone for a while, and to speak more fully yet of the said course of the Church, he bringeth in another pageant (as it were) of seven Angels with \f - \ft \+it Ca. 8. to the 12.\+it*\f* seven Trumpets. The effect of both the Seals & Trumpets, is this: That the Church beginning and proceeding, there should be raised against it, cruel persecutions, and pestilent heresies: and at length after all heresies, a certain most blasphemous Apostasy, being the next preparative to the coming of Antichrist: After all which, Antichrist himself in person shall appear in the time of the sixth seal, and sixth trumpet, persecuting and seducing (for the short time of his reign) more than all before him. The Church not withstanding shall still continue, and wade through all, because Christ her Spouse is stronger than all these adversaries. Who also straight after the said sixth time, shall in the seventh, come in majesty and judge all.
\ip Of the which judgement, deferring yet a while to speak at large, he doth first in the third \f - \ft \+it 3.\+it*\f* \f - \ft \+it C. 12. 13. 14.\+it*\f* part entreat more fully of the Devils working by Antichrist and his company against the Church, that the justice of Christ afterward in judging, may be more manifest.
\ip At length therefore in the fourth part he cometh to the seven last plagues, the seventh of \f - \ft \+it 4.\+it*\f* \f - \ft \+it C. 15. to the 21.\+it*\f* them containing the final damnation of the whole multitude, society or corps of the wicked, from the beginning of the world to the end. Which multitude, in the Gospel and first Epistle of this same S. John (as also in the other Scriptures commonly) is often called \it Mundus, the world.\it* and here \f - \ft \+it 1. Io. 2. Apoc. 17.\+it*\f* he calleth it partly, \it Meretricem, a whore or harlot\it*, because with her concupiscence she enticeth the carnal and earthly men away from God: partly, \it Civitatem Babylon, the City of Babylon\it*, because it maketh war against Hierusalem the city of God, and Laboureth to hold God's people captive in sin, as it was shadowed in Nabuchodonosor & his Babylonians, leading and holding the Jews with their Hierusalem, in captivity, until Cyrus (in figure of Christ) delivered them. But whether all these seven plagues should be understood (as the seventh) of Domesday it-self, it is hard to define. More like it is, that the first six are to go before Domesday: but whether corporally and literally, (so as Moyses plagued Egypt) or rather spiritually, it is more hard to define. Yet it seemeth more easy, to understand them corporally, as also the plagues wherewith Elias and his fellow shall in the time of Antichrist plague the wicked (which peradventure shall be the same last plagues) whereof we read in this book c. 11. v. 6. But not content to have described thus the damnation of the whole adulterous & bloody society, he doth also expressly report of their three grand Captains' damnation, which are these, Antichrist, and his False-prophet, and the Devil himself the author of all this mischief.
\ip Finally, on the other side, in the fifth part he reporteth the unspeakable and everlasting \f - \ft \+it 5.\+it*\f* \f - \ft \+it C. 21. 22.\+it*\f* glory, that the Church after all this suffering, shall by Christ her glorious Spouse be assumpted unto. And so he concludeth the book.
\c 1
\cd 9. S. John being banished in the isle Patmos, is commanded to write to the seven Churches of Asia (signified by the seven candlesticks) that which he saw upon a Sunday, round about the Son of man: 13. whose manner of apparition is described. \f - \ft The 1. part. Seven epistles to the Churches.\f*
\p
\v 1 \sc The\sc* \f 1 \fr 1.1 \ft The Epistle upon Michaelmas day Septemb. 29, & on the Apparition of S. Michael \+it Mai. 8.\+it*\f* Apocalypse of \sc Jesus\sc* Christ which God gave him, to make manifest to his servants the things which must be done quickly: and signified, sending by his Angel to his servant John, \ef - \fr 1.1 \fq Apocalypse. \ft ¹ Of the Apocalypse thus writeth the ancient father Denys, Bishop of Corinth, as Eusebius allegeth him \+it li. 7. c. 20 hist. Eccl.\+it* Of this book (saith he) it is my opinion, that the matter thereof is far more profound than my wit can reach unto, and I doubt not but almost in every sentence of it, there lieth hidden a certain sense exceeding mystical and marvelous, which though I understand not, yet I conceive that under the words there is a deep meaning, and I measure not the matter by reason, but attribute all to faith, taking it to be mystical and divine, that I can by cogitation comprise: not repricing that which I understand not, but therefore I admire with reverence, because my wit cannot attain to it. Again S. Augustine saith, that in the Apocalypse many things are obscurely spoken, to exercise the mind of the reader: and yet some few things left evident, that through them, as one with labours almost and sweat, specially for that which is more excellent: other things in diverse sorts, that seeming to speak of sundry matters, indeed is found but to utter the same things diverse ways. \+it li. 20 de Civit. Dec. 1. 17.\+it* Which we set down here in the beginning, to warn the good Christian reader, to be humble and wise in the reading both of all other holy Scriptures, and specially of this divine and admirable book, and to excite him further to understand these Annotations, according to our former trade and purpose, only or chiefly note unto the studious, such places as may be used by Catholics, or abused by Heretics, in the controversies of this time, and some other that have special matter of edification, and that as briefly as may be, for that the volume growth great. \fq ¹ \ft An admonition to the reader concerning the difficulty of this book.\ef*
\v 2 who hath given testimony to the word of God, and the testimony of \sc Jesus\sc* Christ, what things soever he hath seen.
\v 3 Blessed is he that readeth and heareth the words of this prophecy: and \f 1 \fr 1.3 \ft There be many (specially now a days) that be great readers, hearers and talkers of Scriptures, but that is not enough to make them good or blessed before God, except they keep the things prescribed and taught therein, according to our Saviour's saying \+it (Luc. 11.)\+it* Blessed are they that hear the word of God, and keep it.\f* keepeth those things which be written in it. For the time is nigh.
\v 4 John to the seven churches which are in Asia. Grace to you and peace from \x - \xt Exo. 3, 14.\x* him that is, and that was, and that shall come, and from the seven spirits which are in the sight of his throne, \ef - \fr 1.4 \fq To the 7 Churches. \ft ¹ That certain number may be observed as significative and mystical: it is plain by many places of holy Scripture, and by the ancient Doctors' special noting of the same to many purposes. ² Whereby we see the rashness of our Adversaries, in condemning generally all religious respect of certain numbers in our prayers, fasts, or actions. Namely the number of Seven, is mystical, and prophetical, perfect, and holy (as S. Augustine saith) the Church knoweth by the Scriptures, to be specially dedicated to the Holy Ghost: and to appertain to spiritual mundation, as in the Prophet's appointing of Naaman to wash seven times in Jordan, and the sprinkling of the blood seven times against the tabernacle. \+it li. 4. quaest. in numer. q. 33. See li. 5. c. 3 de Gen. ad lit. 9. li. 4. quaest in Deuter. q. 42.\+it* All these accord upon Seven. Angels, seven stars, seven spirits, seven candlesticks, seven lamps, seven trumpets, seven vials, seven horns of the Lamb, seven hills, seven thunders, seven heads of the Dragon, signifying the Devil: seven of the beast that is Antichrist. This number also of the visions is specially marked to be seven, in this book, and every time that this number is used in this prophecy, it hath a mystery & a more large meaning, than the nature of that number is precisely and vulgarly taken for. As when he writeth to seven Churches, it is to be understood of all the Churches in the world, as the seven Angels, for all the Angels, the numbers of the whole Catholic Church: and so forth in the rest, because the number of Seven, hath the perfection of universality in it, as S. Augustine saith \+it li. 5 quaest. in Deuter. q. 42.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Numbers mystical. \fq ² \ft The number of seven: specially in this book.\ef* \ef - \fr 1.4 \fq From the 7 spirits. \ft ¹ The Holy Ghost may be here meant, and so called for his sevenfold gifts or graces, as some expounders think, but it seemeth more probable that he speaketh of the holy Angels, by comparing this to the like in the 5 Chapter following: where he seemeth to call these, the seven spirits sent into the world. \+it v. 6.\+it* ² and ³ S. Paul to the Galatians, \+it ch. 3.\+it* as S. Augustine saith, and oft the Protestants take it in their commentaries: which we note, because thereupon they must needs confess that the Apostle here giveth or wisheth grace, not from God only, but also from his Angels: that all benediction cometh of God, and necessarily of God and Angels by him, and of Saints, being but his creatures. And so they may learn, that the faithful often joining in one speech God and our Lady, our Lord and any of his Saints, to help us to bless us, is not superstitious, but an Apostolical speech. And so the Patriarch said \+it (Gen. 48. v. 16.)\+it* The Angel that delivereth me from all evils, bless these children. \+it See the Annot. Act. 18, 28.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Grace & peace from God and the holy Angels. \fq ² \ft Bishops and Priests are called Angels. \fq ³ \ft \+it Mal. 2. 7.\+it*\ef*
\v 5 and from \sc Jesus\sc* Christ who is the faithful witness, the \x - \xt Col. 1.\x* First-born of the dead, and the Prince of the kings of the earth, who hath loved us, and \x - \xt Heb. 9. 1. Pet. 1.\x* washed us from our sins in his blood,
\v 6 and hath made us \x - \xt 1. Pet. 1.\x* a Kingdom and Priests to God and his Father, to him be glory and empire for ever and ever. Amen. \ef - \fr 1.6 \fq All kingdoms and Priests. \ft ¹ As all that truly serve God, & overcome their concupiscences and whatsoever would induce them to sin, be kings: so all that employ their works and themselves to serve God & offer all their actions as an acceptable sacrifice to him, be priests. Nevertheless, as if any man would therefore affirm that there ought to be no other earthly powers or kings to govern in worldly affairs over Christians, he were a seditious Heretic, even so are they that upon this or the like places where all Christians be called priests in a spiritual sort, would therefore infer, that there are or ought to be no ecclesiastical Priests, such as are ordained, or that there ought to be none but such spiritual priests, for it is the seditious voice of Core, saying to Moyses and Aaron, \+it Let it suffice you, that all the multitude is of holy ones, and the Lord is in them: Why are you exalted over the people of the Lord?\+it* \+it Num. 16.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft How all Christians be both Kings & Priests.\ef*
\v 7 Behold he cometh with the clouds, and every eye shall see him, and \x - \xt Zach. 12.\x* they that pricked him. And all the tribes of the earth shall bewail themselves upon him. Yea, Amen.
\v 8 \x - \xt Esa. 44. Apo. 21. 22. 13.\x* I am Alpha and Omega, the beginning and end, saith our Lord God, which is, and which was, and which shall come, the Omnipotent.
\v 9 I John your brother and partaker in tribulation, and the Kingdom, and patience in Christ \sc Jesus\sc*, was in \f 1 \fr 1.9 \ft Banished thither for religion by Nero, or rather by Domitian, almost \+it 60.\+it* years after Christ's Ascension.\f* the Island, which is called Patmos, for the word of God and the testimony of \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 10 I was \f 1 \fr 1.10 \ft c I had a vision, & not with my corporal eyes, but in spirit I beheld the similitudes of the things following.\f* in spirit on the Dominical day, and heard behind me a great voice as it were of a trumpet \ef - \fr 1.10 \fq On the Dominical day. \ft ¹ Many notable points may be marked here: First, that even in the Apostles' time there were days deputed to the service of God, and so made holy and different, though not by nature, yet by use and benediction, from other profane or (as we call them) work-days. ² Secondly, that the Apostles and faithful abrogated the Sabbath which was the seventh day, and made holy day for it, the next day following, being the eighth day in count from the creation: and that without all Scripture, or commandment of Christ that we read of, yea (which is more) not only otherwise preserved, but fully observed on that very authority only: which sheweth that not the Church alone without Scripture hath authority to appoint holy days. And God himself in the second commandment, yea and otherwise than he ordained in the first creation, when he sanctified precisely the Sabbath day, & not the day following, gave he the Holy Ghost to be resident in the Church, to guide it into all truths. ³ As Saturday was in memory of the creation, so now Sunday is in memory of Christ's Resurrection and triumph over death, and the coming of the Holy Ghost. ⁴ The ancient Church called Sunday, \+it Dies Dominica\+it*, the Lord's day: and all other feasts, \+it Feriae\+it*: and the Seventh day still, \+it Sabbathum\+it* the Sabbath: not by the Pagan names of planets, as in the Roman's time: some by the name of certain Idols that then Saxons did worship, & to which they dedicated their days before they were Christians. Which names the Church useth not, but hath appointed to call the first day, the \+it Dominica\+it*, after the Apostle here: the other by the names of \+it Feria\+it*, until the last. Lastly observe, that God revealeth such great things to Prophets, rather upon holy days, and in ⁵ times of contemplation, sacrifice, and prayer, than on other profane days. And therefore as S. Peter \+it (Act. 10)\+it* had a revelation at the sixth hour of prayer, and Zacharias \+it (Luc. 1)\+it* at the hour of the ninth, and Cornelius \+it (Act. 10)\+it* when he was at his prayers the ninth hour, so here S. John noteth that he had all these marvelous visions upon a Sunday. \fq ¹ \ft Sunday made holiday by the Apostles & the Church's authority. \fq ² \ft Other feasts ordained by the Church. \fq ³ \ft As Saturday was in memory of the creation, so Sunday of Christ's resurrection. \fq ⁴ \ft The church useth not the Heathenish names of days: but \+it Dies Dominica, feriae, Sabbathum.\+it* \fq ⁵ \ft God giveth greater grace at holy times of prayer and fasting.\ef*
\v 11 saying: That which thou seest, write in a book: and send to the seven churches which are in Asia, to Ephesus, and Smyrna, and Pergamus, and Thiatira, and Sardis, and Philadelphia, and Laodicia.
\v 12 \f 1 \fr 1.12 \ft b The 1. GENERAL VISION of the 7 according to S. Ambrose.\f* And I turned, to see the voice that spake with me. And being turned I saw seven candlesticks of gold:
\v 13 and in the midst of the seven candlesticks of gold, one \x - \xt c podere Sap. 18. 24.\x* like to the Son of man, vested in a \f 2 \fr 1.13 \ft It seemeth not to be Christ himself; but an Angel bearing Christ's person; & using diverse speeches proper to Christ.\f* priestly garment to the foot, & girded about near to the paps with a girdle of gold. \ef - \fr 1.13 \fq Vested in a Priestly garment. \ft ¹ He appeared in a large garment or vestment proper unto Priests (for so it was, \+it Poderes,\+it* doth signify) and that was most agreeable for him that represented the person of Christ the high Priest, and appeared to John being a most holy Priest, and who is specially noted in the Ecclesiastical history for his Priestly garment called, \+it petalon\+it* or \+it lamina. Euseb. li. 3 hist. Eccl. c. 25. & li. 5. c. 23.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Priestly garments.\ef*
\v 14 And his head & hairs were white, as white wool, and as snow, and his eyes as the flame of fire.
\v 15 And his feet like to latten, as in a burning furnace. And his voice as the voice of many waters:
\v 16 and he had in his right hand seven stars. And from his mouth proceeded a sharp two-edged sword: and his face, as the sun shineth in his virtue.
\v 17 And when I had seen him, I fell at his feet as dead. And he put his right hand upon me, saying: Fear not. \x - \xt Esa. 41, 4. 44, 6.\x* I am the First and the Last,
\v 18 and alive, and was dead, and behold I am living for ever and ever, and have the keys of death and of hell.
\v 19 Write therefore the things which thou hast seen, and that are, and that must be done after these:
\v 20 The Sacrament of the seven stars, which thou hast seen in my right hand, and the seven candlesticks of Gold. The seven stars, are the Angels of the seven Churches. And the seven candlesticks, are \f 1 \fr 1.20 \ft S. Irenæus alluding to this saith, \+it The Church everywhere preacheth the truth, & this is the seven-fold candlestick, bearing the light of Christ\+it* &c. Li. \+it 5. advers. hær.\+it*\f* the seven Churches. \ef - \fr 1.20 \fq The seven stars. \ft The stars are the of the Church, as the Churches themselves are the golden candlesticks of the world: ¹ no doubt to signify, that Christ preserveth the truth only in and by the lawful Bishops and Catholic Church, and that Christ's truth is not to be sought for, in corners or conventicles of Heretics, but at the Bishop's hands, and ² upon the candlestick which shineth to all in the house. \fq ¹ \ft The true religion manifest as the light on a candlestick. \fq ² \ft \+it Mt. 5. 15.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 1.20 \fq The Angels of the Churches. \ft ¹ The whole Church of Christ hath S. Michael for her keeper and Protector, and therefore keepeth his holy day only by name, among all Angels. And as earthly kingdoms have their special Angels Protectors, as we see in the 10 Chapter of Daniel: so much the more particular Churches of Christendom. See S. Hieron in \+it 34 Ezech.\+it* But of those Angels it is not here meant, as is manifest. ² And therefore Angels here must needs signify the Priests or Bishops specially of the Churches here, and in them, all the governors of the whole & of every particular Church of Christendom. They are called Angels, for that they are God's messengers to us, interpreters of his will, our keepers and directors in religion, our intercessors, the carriers and offerers of our prayers to him, and mediators unto him under Christ, and for these causes and for their great dignity they are here and in ³ other places of Scripture called Angels. \fq ¹ \ft Angels Protectors. \fq ² \ft Bishops and Priests are called Angels. \fq ³ \ft \+it Mal. 2. 7.\+it*\ef*
\c 2
\cd He is commanded to write diverse things to the churches of Ephesus, Smyrna, Pergamus, and Thyatira: praising them that had not admitted the doctrine of the Heretics called Nicolaitæ, 22. and calling others by threats unto penance: 26. and promising reward to him that manfully overcometh.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \f 1 \fr 2.1 \ft That which before he willed him to write to the Church, he now willeth to be written to the Angels or Bishops of the same only. Where we see, it is all one, to the Church, and to the head or governor thereof.\f* to the Angel of the Church of Ephesus write, Thus saith he which holdeth the seven stars in his right hand, which walketh in the midst of the seven candlesticks of gold: \ef - \fr 2.1 \fq Holdeth the seven. \ft Much to be observed, that Christ hath such care over the Church and the Bishops thereof, ¹ that he is said here to bear them up in his right hand, and to walk in the middes of them: no doubt to uphold and preserve them and to guide them in all truth. \fq ¹ \ft Christ's care of his Church.\ef*
\v 2 I know thy works and labour, and thy patience: & that thou canst not bear evil men, & hast tried them which say themselves to be Apostles, and are not, and hast found them liars: \ef - \fr 2.2 \fq Thy works, labour, patience, &c. \ft Things required in a Bishop. First, good works, and great patience in tribulation. Next, ¹ zeal and sharp discipline toward offenders is here commended in them. Thirdly, wisdom & diligence in trial of false Apostles and preachers coming in sheep-skins: where is signified the watchful providence that ought to be in them, that Heretics enter not into their flocks. \fq ¹ \ft Special virtues required in a Bishop.\ef*
\v 3 and thou hast patience, and hast borne for my name, and hast not fainted.
\v 4 But I have against thee a few things, because \f 1 \fr 2.4 \ft By this we see is plainly refuted that which some Heretics hold, that a man once in grace or charity can never fall from it.\f* thou hast left thy first charity.
\v 5 Be mindful therefore from whence thou art fallen: and do penance, and do the first works. But if not: I come to thee, & will move thy candlestick out of his place, unless thou do penance. \ef - \fr 2.5 \fq Will move. \ft ¹ Note that the cause why God taketh the truth from certain countries, and removeth their Bishops or Churches into captivity or desolation, is the sin of the Prelates and people. And that is the cause (no doubt) that Christ hath taken away our golden candlestick, that is, our Church in England. God grant us to remember our fall, to do penance and the former works of charity which our first Bishops and Church were notable and renowned for. \fq ¹ \ft Sin is the cause that God taketh the Cath. faith from countries.\ef*
\v 6 But this thou hast, because thou hatest the facts of the Nicolaites, which I also hate. \ef - \fr 2.6 \fq Because thou hatest. \ft We see here that of all things, Christian people (specially Bishops) should have great zeal against Heretics and hate them, ¹ that is, their wicked doctrine and conditions, even as God hateth them. For which only zeal, our Lord saith here that he beareth with some Churches and Prelates, and saveth them from perishing. \fq ¹ \ft Zeal against Heretics.\ef* \ef - \fr 2.6 \fq Of the Nicolaites. \ft Heretics have their callings of certain persons, as is noted at large \+it Act. 11. 26.\+it* These had their name of Nicolas, ¹ one of the 7 first Deacons that were chosen \+it Act. 6.\+it* Who is thought to have taught community of women or wives, and that it was lawful to eat of meats offered to idols. Which later point is such a thing, as if one should hold it lawful to receive the bread or wine of the new Communion, which is a kind of \+it Idolothyta\+it*, that is, \+it idolatrous meats\+it*. For though such creatures be good by creation, yet they be made execrable by profane blessings of Heretics or Idolaters. And concerning the name of Nicolaites given here by our Lord himself to those Heretics, it is a very pattern and mark unto the faithful for ever. What kind of men they should be, that should be called after the like sort, Arians, Macedonians, Nestorians, Lutherans, Zuinglians, &c. See S, Hierom \+it cont. Lucifer. in fine.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Nicolaites the first Heretics so called, as a pattern of Arians, Lutherans, and the like peculiar callings.\ef*
\v 7 He that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith to the Churches: To him that overcometh, I will give to eat of the tree of life, which is in the Paradise of my God.
\v 8 And to the Angel of the Church of Smyrna write: Thus saith \x - \xt Apoc. 1, 17.\x* the First and the Last, who was dead, and liveth:
\v 9 I know thy tribulation and thy \f 1 \fr 2.9 \ft This Church representeth the state of them that are spoiled of their goods, imprisoned, & manifoldly afflicted for the Catholic faith.\f* poverty, but thou art rich: and thou art blasphemed of them that say themselves to be Jews and are not, but are the synagogue of Satan.
\v 10 Fear none of these things which thou shalt suffer. Behold the devil will send some of you into prison that you may be tried: and you shall have tribulation ten days. Be thou faithful until death: and I will give thee \f 1 \fr 2.10 \ft The singular reward of Martyrdom.\f* the crown of life.
\v 11 He that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith to the Churches: He that shall overcome, shall not be hurt of the \f 1 \fr 2.11 \ft The death of the body is the first death: the death of the soul, the second, which Martyrs are surest to escape of all men.\f* second death.
\v 12 And to the Angel of the Church of Pergamus write: Thus saith he that hath the sharp two-edged sword:
\v 13 I know where thou dwellest, where the seat of Satan is: and thou holdest my name, and hast not denied my faith. And in those days Antipas my faithful witness, who was slain among you, \f 1 \fr 2.13 \ft The special residence of Satan is where the faithful are persecuted for Christ's truth. Where not to deny the Cath. faith for fear, is much here commended.\f* where Satan dwelleth.
\v 14 But I have against thee a few things: because thou hast there, them that hold the doctrine of Balaam, who taught Balac \x - \xt Nu. 24, 14. 25, 2.\x* to cast a scandal before the children of Israel, to eat and commit fornication: \ef - \fr 2.14 \fq To cast a scandal. \ft ¹ Josephus writeth that when Balaam could not curse God's people, nor otherwise annoy them, he taught Balac a way how to overthrow them: ² to wit, by presenting unto them their Heathen women very beautiful, and delicate dishes of meat offered to Beelphegor: that so being tempted they might fall to heathenish manners and displease God. ³ To which crafty counsel of Balaam the Apostle resembleth Heretic's fraud, who by offering of liberty of meat, women, Church goods, breach of vows, and such other licentious allurements, cause many more to fall, than by their preaching. \fq ¹ \ft \+it li. 4. Antiq. c. 6.\+it* \fq ² \ft Balaam overcoming God's people by persuasion of lechery and bellycheer, was a type of Heretics. \fq ³ \ft \+it 2. Pet. 2, 15. Iuda v. 11.\+it*\ef*
\v 15 so hast thou also them that hold the doctrine of the Nicolaites.
\v 16 In like manner do penance. If not: I will come to thee quickly, and will fight against them with the sword of my mouth.
\v 17 He that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith to the Churches: To him that over cometh I will give the hidden manna, and will give him a \f 1 \fr 2.17 \ft \+it c ψῆφον calculum\+it*\f* white counter, and in the counter, a new name written, which no man knoweth, but he that receiveth it.
\v 18 And to the Angel of the Church of Thyatira write: Thus saith the Son of God, which hath eyes as a flame of fire, and his feet like to latten.
\v 19 I know thy \f 1 \fr 2.19 \ft None of these are any thing worth without the other.\f* works, and faith, and thy charity, and ministry, and thy patience and thy last works more than the former.
\v 20 But I have against thee a few things: because thou permittest the \x - \xt 3. Reg. 18.\x* woman Jezabel, who calleth herself a prophetess, to teach, and to seduce my servants, to fornicate, and to eat of things sacrificed to idols. \ef - \fr 2.20 \fq The woman Jezabel. \ft He warneth Bishops to be zealous and stout against false Prophets and Heretics of what sort soever, ¹ by alluding covertly to the example of holy Elias that in zeal killed 450 false prophets of Jezabel, ² and spared not Achab nor Jezabel themselves, ³ but told them to their faces that they troubled Israel, that is, the faithful people of God. And whether there were any such great woman then, a furtherer and promoter of the Nicolaites, who the Prophet should here mean, it is hard to say. \fq ¹ \ft Zeal against Heretics. \fq ² \ft \+it 3. Reg. 18.\+it* \fq ³ \ft Achab and Jezabel.\ef*
\v 21 And I gave her a time that she might do penance: & she will not repent from her fornication. \ef - \fr 2.21 \fq She will not repent. \ft ¹ See free will here most plainly, and that God is not the proper cause of obduration or impenitence, but man himself only. Our Lord giveth sinners so long life, specially to expect their amendment: but Jezabel (to whom the Apostle here alludeth) would never repent. \fq ¹ \ft Free-will. God is not author of evil.\ef*
\v 22 Behold I will cast her into a bed: and they that commit adultery with her, shall be in very great tribulation, unless they do penance from their works: \ef - \fr 2.22 \fq They that commit adultery with her. \ft ¹ Such as communicate with Heretics, shall be damned (alas) with them. For, not only such as were in their hearts, of Jezabel's religion, or inwardly believed in Baal, but such as externally for fear worshipped him (which the Scriptures call, bowing of their knees to Baal) ² are culpable. As now many bow their knees to the Communion, that bow not their hearts. \fq ¹ \ft They that communicate with Heretic, shall be damned with them. \fq ² \ft \+it 3. Reg. 19. v. 18. Ro. 11.\+it*\ef*
\v 23 and her children I will kill with death, & all the Churches shall know \x - \xt 1. Reg. 16. Ps. 7, 10. Ier. 11, 20.\x* that I am he that searcheth the reins & hearts, & I will give to every one of you \f 2 \fr 2.23 \ft Who seeth not here that good works deserve salvation, as ill works damnation: and that it is not faith alone that God rewardeth, but that faith which worketh by charity?\f* according to his works.
\v 24 But I say to you and the rest which are at Thyatira, whosoever have not this doctrine, which have not known the \f 1 \fr 2.24 \fq depth \ft \+it depths\+it*\f* depth of Satan, as they say, I will not cast upon you another weight.
\v 25 Yet that which you have, hold till I come.
\v 26 And he that shall overcome and keep my works unto the end, I will give him power over the nations, \ef - \fr 2.26 \fq I will give him power. \ft Observe that not only Angels have power and regiment over Countries under God, but now for the honour of Christ's human nature, and for his ministry in the world, the Saints deceased also, ¹ being in heaven, have government over men and Provinces, and therefore have to do with our affairs in the world. Which is against the Heretics of these days, that to take away our prayers to Saints, would spoil them of many sovereign dignities, wherein the Scriptures make them equal with Angels. \fq ¹ \ft Saints also are Patrons, not only Angels.\ef*
\v 27 and \x - \xt Ps. 2, 9.\x* he shall rule them with a rod of iron, and as the vessel of a potter shall they be broken,
\v 28 \f 1 \fr 2.28 \ft This great privilege of Saints riseth of the power and preeminence of Christ, which his father gave him according to his humanity; and therefore to deny it to Saints, is to\f* as I also have received of my Father: and I will give him the morning star.
\v 29 He that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith to the Churches.
\c 3
\cd He is commanded to write to the Churches of Sardis, Philadelphia, and Laodicia: recalling them that err to penance by threatening, but praising the rest, and promising reward to him that overcometh: 15. detesting also the cold indifferent Christians. 20. He saith that God knocketh at the door of men's hearts by offering his grace, for to enter in to him that will open unto him by consent of free will.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* to the Angel of the Church of Sardis, write: Thus saith he that hath the seven Spirits of God, and the seven stars: I know thy works, that thou hast the name that thou livest, and thou art dead.
\v 2 Be vigilant, and confirm the rest of the things which were to die. For I find not thy works full before my God.
\v 3 Have in mind therefore in what manner thou hast received and heard: and keep, and do penance. If therefore thou watch not, \x - \xt 1. Thes. 5, 2. 2. Pet. 3, 10. Apo. 16, 15.\x* I will come to thee as a thief, and thou shalt not know what hour I will come to thee.
\v 4 But thou hast a few names in Sardis, \f 1 \fr 3.4 \ft c Such as have not committed deadly sin after Baptism.\f* which have not defiled their garments: and they shall walk with me in whites, because they \f 2 \fr 3.4 \ft note that there is in man a worthiness of the joys of heaven, by holy life: and this is a common speech in holy Scripture, that man is worthy of God, of heaven, of salvation.\f* are worthy.
\v 5 He that shall overcome, shall thus be vested in white garments, and I will not put his name out of the book of life, and I will confess his name before my Father, and before his Angels. \ef - \fr 3.5 \fq He that shall overcome. \ft In all these speeches to diverse Bishops and their Churches, ¹ he continually encourageth them to constancy in faith and good life, by setting before their eyes the reward of the next life. And yet the Calvinists would have no man do good in respect of such reward. \fq ¹ \ft Doing well in respect of reward.\ef*
\v 6 He that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith to the Churches.
\v 7 And to the Angel of the Church of Philadelphia write: Thus saith the Holy one and the True one, he that hath the \x - \xt Esa. 22, 22.\x* key of David; he that openeth, and no man shutteth, shutteth, and no man openeth:
\v 8 I know thy works. Behold I have given before thee a door opened which no man can shut: because thou hast a little power, and hast kept my word, and hast not denied my name.
\v 9 Behold I will give of the synagogue of Satan, which say they be Jews, and are not, but do lie. Behold I will make them come and adore before thy feet. And they shall know that I have loved thee. \ef - \fr 3.9 \fq Adore before thy feet. \ft ¹ You see this word of adoration is in Scriptures used for worship of creatures also, and that to fall before the feet of holy men or Angels for duty and reverence, is not idolatry, except the proper honour due to God, be given unto them. See the Annotations upon the 19 & 22 Chapter concerning the Apostle's prostration before the Angel. And the Adversaries evasion, saying that the adoration was of God only: and that, \+it before the feet\+it* of the party, signifieth nothing else but, \+it in his presence\+it*, is false and against the phrase of Scriptures. \+it 23 4 Reg. 4.\+it* where the Sunamite adored Elisæus, falling down before his feet, and \+it 4 Reg. 2.\+it* the sons of the Prophets adored him in the same sort. And here this adoration cannot be meant but of the Bishop or Angel of Philadelphia, because he promiseth this honour as a reward, and as an effect of his love towards him, saying, \+it And they shall know that I have loved thee.\+it* And that which he saith in the 22 Chapter, \+it I fell down to adore before the feet of the Angel\+it*: the very same he expresseth thus in the 19 Chapter, \+it I fell before his feet to adore him:\+it* making it all one, to adore before his feet, and to adore him. \fq ¹ \ft Adoration of creatures, called Dulia.\ef*
\v 10 Because thou hast kept the word of my patience, and I will keep thee from the hour of tentation, which shall come upon the whole world to tempt the inhabitants on the earth.
\v 11 Behold I come quickly: hold that which thou hast, that no man take thy crown. \ef - \fr 3.11 \fq That no man take thy crown. \ft That is, his crown of everlasting life and glory, if he persevere not to the end in faith & good works: ¹ otherwise another shall enter into his place, as Mathias did both to the dignity of the Apostleship, & to the heavenly crown due for the well using and executing of the same function: which Judas might and should have had, if he had persevered to the end. And as the Gentiles came into the grace and place of the Jews. Other difficulties concerning this kind of speech are resolved in Schoolmen, and are not here to be stood upon. \fq ¹ \ft Perseverance in good & continuing to the end.\ef*
\v 12 He that shall overcome, I will make him a pillar in the temple of my God: and he shall go out no more: and I will write upon him the name of my God, and the name of the city of my God, new Jerusalem, which descendeth out of Heaven from my God, and my new name.
\v 13 He that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith to the Churches.
\v 14 And to the Angel of the Church of Laodicia write: Thus saith \f 1 \fr 3.14 \ft c ὁ ἀμήν\f* Amen, the faithful and true witness, \f 2 \fr 3.14 \ft Ecclesiastici 24, 9. 14. Col. 1, 15.\f* which is the beginning of the creature of God:
\v 15 I know thy works, that thou art neither cold, nor hot. I would thou were cold, or hot.
\v 16 But because thou art lukewarm, and neither cold nor hot, I will begin to vomit thee out of my mouth. \ef - \fr 3.16 \fq Lukewarm. \ft Zeal and fervour is commendable, specially in God's cause: ¹ and the Neuters that be neither hot nor cold, are to Christ and his Church burdenous and loathsome, as lukewarm water is to a man's stomach, provoking him to vomit, and therefore he threateneth to void up such Neuters out of his mouth. \fq ¹ \ft Neuters or indifferents in religion.\ef*
\v 17 Because thou sayest, That I am rich, and enriched, and lack nothing; and knowest not that thou art a miser, and miserable, and poor, and blind, and naked.
\v 18 I counsel thee to buy of me gold fire-tried, that thou mayest be made rich: and mayest be clothed in white garments, that the confusion of thy nakedness appear not: and with eye-salve anoint thine eyes, that thou mayest see.
\v 19 I, \x - \xt Prov. 3, 12. Heb. 12, 6.\x* whom I love, do rebuke and chastise. Be zealous therefore and do penance.
\v 20 Behold I stand at the door and \f 1 \fr 3.20 \ft God first calleth upon man and knocketh at the door of his heart: that is to say, offereth his grace. And it lieth in man to give consent by free-will holpen also by his grace.\f* knock. If any man shall hear my voice, and open to me the gate, I will enter in to him, and will sup with him, and he with me.
\v 21 He that shall overcome, I will give unto him to sit with me in my throne: as I also have overcome, and have sitten with my Father in his throne.
\v 22 He that hath an ear, let him hear what the Spirit saith to the Churches.
\c 4
\cd 1. A door being open in heaven, he saw one sitting in a throne, 4. and round about him four and twenty seniors sitting, 6. and the four beasts here described, 9. which with the 24 seniors continually glorified him that sat in the throne. \f - \ft The 2. part. First, the book with 7 seals. Secondly, 7 Angels with trumpets.\f*
\p
\v 1 \sc After\sc* \f 1 \fr 4.1 \ft THE 2. VISION. In which is represented unto us the glory and majesty of God in heaven, and the incessant honour & praises of all Angels and Saints assisting him. Which is resembled in the daily honour done to him by all orders and sorts of holy men in the Church militant also.\f* these things I looked, & behold a door open in heaven, and the first voice which I heard, was as it were of a trumpet speaking with me, saying: Come up hither, and I will shew thee the things which must be done quickly after these.
\v 2 And immediately I was in spirit: and behold there was a seat set in heaven, and upon the seat one sitting.
\v 3 And he that sat, was like in sight, to the Jasper stone, and the Sardine: and there was a rain-bow round about the seat, like to the sight of an Emerald.
\v 4 And round about the seat, four and twenty seats: and upon the thrones, four and twenty Seniors sitting, clothed about in white garments, and on their heads crowns of gold.
\v 5 And from the throne proceeded lightnings, and voices, and thunders: and seven lamps burning before the throne, which are the seven Spirits of God.
\v 6 And in the sight of the seat, as it were a sea of glass like to crystal: and in the midst of the seat and round about the seat \f 1 \fr 4.6 \ft These four beasts, and the like described \+it Ezech. 1.\+it* by the judgement of the holy Doctors signify the 4 Evangelists, and in them all true Preachers. The man, Matthew, the lion, Mark: the calf, Luke: the eagle, John. See the causes hereof in the Sum of the 4 Evangelists. \+it pag. 1. S. Grego. in 1. Ezech.\+it*\f* four beasts full of eyes before and behind.
\v 7 And the first beast, like to a lion: and the second beast, like to a calf, and the third beast, having the face as it were of a man: and the fourth beast, like to an eagle flying.
\v 8 And the four beasts, every one of them had six wings round about: and within they are full of eyes. And they had no rest day and night, saying, \it Holy, Holy, Holy, Lord God omnipotent,\it* \x - \xt Esa. 6, 3.\x* which was, and which is, and which shall come. \ef - \fr 4.8 \fq Holy, holy, holy. \ft ¹ This word is thrice repeated here, and \+it Esa. 6\+it*: and to the imitation thereof, in the service of the holy Church, at \+it Te Deum\+it*, and at Mass, specially in the Preface next before the great mysteries, for the honour of the three persons in the B. Trinity, and that the Church militant may join with the triumphant, and with all the orders of Angels, who also are present at the consecration, and do service there to our common Lord and Master, as S. Chrysostom writeth \+it li. 6 de Sacerdotio.\+it* and \+it ho. 1. de verb. Esa. to. 1.\+it* The Greeks call it, the hymn \+it Trisagios\+it*, that is, \+it Thrice holy\+it*. \fq ¹ \ft The \+it Sanctus\+it* thrice repeated.\ef*
\v 9 And when those beasts gave glory and honour and benediction to him that sitteth upon the throne, that liveth for ever and ever:
\v 10 the four and twenty Seniors fell down before him that sitteth in the throne, & adored him that liveth for ever and ever, and cast their crowns before the throne, saying:
\v 11 Thou art worthy O Lord our God to receive glory and honour and power: because thou hast created all things, and for thy will they were and have been created.
\c 5
\cd 4. S. John weeping, because no man could open the book sealed with seven seals: 6. the Lamb that was slain, opened it: which being done, the four beasts and four and twenty seniors, with an innumerable multitude of Angels & all creatures, did glorify him exceedingly.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \f 1 \fr 5.1 \ft THE 3 VISION.\f* I saw in the right hand of him that sat upon the throne, a \f 2 \fr 5.1 \ft S. Gregory taketh it to be the book of holy Scripture \+it li. 4. Dialog. c. 42.\+it*\f* book written within and without, sealed with seven seals.
\v 2 And I saw a strong Angel, preaching with a loud voice: Who is worthy to open the book, & to loose the seals thereof?
\v 3 And no man was able neither in heaven nor in earth, nor \f 1 \fr 5.3 \ft He speaketh not of the damned in Hell, of whom there could be no question: but of the faithful in Abraham's bosom; & in Purgatory.\f* under the earth, to open the book, nor look on it.
\v 4 And I wept much because no man was found worthy to open the book, nor to see it.
\v 5 And one of the Seniors said to me: Weep not; behold \x - \xt Gen. 49, 9.\x* the \f 2 \fr 5.5 \ft c So did Jacob \+it (Gen. 49.)\+it* call Christ, for his kingly fortitude in subduing the world unto him.\f* Lion of the Tribe of Juda, the root of David, hath won, to open the book, and to loose the seven seals thereof.
\v 6 \f 1 \fr 5.6 \ft b The Epistle upon All-Hallows eve.\f* And I saw, and behold in the midst of the throne and of the four beasts and in the midst of the Seniors, \f 2 \fr 5.6 \ft So Christ is called for that he is the immaculate host or sacrifice for our sins.\f* a Lamb standing as it were slain, having seven horns & seven eyes: which are the seven Spirits of God, sent into all the earth.
\v 7 And he came, and received the book out of the right hand of him that sat in the throne.
\v 8 And when he had opened the book, the four beasts and the four and twenty Seniors fell before the Lamb, having every one harps, and golden vials full of odours, which are the prayers of saints: \ef - \fr 5.8 \fq The prayers of Saints. \ft Hereby it is plain that the Saints in heaven offer up the prayers of faithful and holy persons in earth (called here saints, and in Scripture often) unto Christ. ¹ And among so many divine & unsearchable mysteries set down without exposition, it pleased God yet, that the Apostle himself should open this one point unto us, that these odours be the lauds and prayers of the faithful, ascending and offered up to God as incense, by the Saints in heaven. That so the Protestants may have no excuse of their error. That the Saints have no knowledge of our affairs or desires. \fq ¹ \ft The Saints in heaven offer our prayers to God.\ef*
\v 9 And they sang a new canticle, saying: Thou art worthy o Lord to take the book, and to open the seals thereof: \f 1 \fr 5.9 \ft This maketh against the Calvinists who are not content to say that we merit not, but that Christ merited not for himself. Calv. \+it Philip. 2. v. 9.\+it*\f* because thou wast slain, and hast redeemed us to God in thy blood out of every tribe and tongue and people and nation,
\v 10 and \x - \xt 1. Pet. 2.\x* hast made us to our God \f 2 \fr 5.10 \fq a Kingdom \ft \+it kings\+it*\f* a Kingdom and Priests, and we shall reign upon the earth. \ef - \fr 5.10 \fq A kingdom and priests. \ft To serve God and subdue vices and sins, is to reign or to be a king spiritually. ¹ Likewise to offer unto him the sacrifices of good works, is to be a priest after a sort: though neither the one nor the other in proper speech. See the Annotation before Chap. 1. v. 6. \fq ¹ \ft Spiritual kings and Priests.\ef*
\v 11 \x - \xt Dan. 7. 10.\x* And I looked, and heard the voice of many Angels round about the throne, and of the beasts & of the Seniors: and the number of them was \f 2 \fr 5.11 \ft The Epistle in a votive Mass of the holy Angels.\f* thousands of thousands,
\v 12 saying with a loud voice: The Lamb that was slain, is worthy to receive power, and \f 1 \fr 5.12 \fq divinity \ft \+it riches\+it*\f* divinity, and wisdom, and strength, and honour, and glory, and benediction.
\v 13 And every creature that is in heaven, and upon the earth, and under the earth, and that are in the sea, and that are therein: all did I hear saying: \x - \xt Apoc. 4, 11.\x* To him that sitteth in the throne, and \f 2 \fr 5.13 \ft All the said creatures are bound to give honour, not only to God, but to Christ as man, and our Redeemer: & so they here do.\f* to the Lamb, benediction and honour and glory and power for ever and ever. \ef - \fr 5.13 \fq Every creature. \ft He meaneth the creatures in heaven, as Angels and Saints, the holy persons in earth, and those that were in Limbo, ¹ or be in Purgatory (for of the damned in hell he cannot speak in his case:) lastly, of the peoples in Islands (here called the sea) which the Prophets use often to name severally, when they foretell the spreading of Christ's glory through the world. as Esa. c. 49. \+it Hear ye Islands and you people a far off. &c.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft \+it Limbus Patrum\+it* and Purgatory.\ef*
\v 14 And the four beasts said, Amen. And the four and twenty Seniors fell on their faces: and adored him that liveth for ever and ever.
\c 6
\cd 8. Four seals of the seven being opened, there follow diverse effects against the earth. 9. when the fifth seal was opened, the souls of martyrs desire that the judgement may be hastened: 12. and at the opening of the sixth, there are signs shewed of the judgement to come.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* I saw that the Lamb had opened one of the seven seals, and I heard one of the four beasts, saying, as it were the voice of thunder: Come, and see.
\v 2 And I saw: And behold a white horse, and he that sat upon him had a bow, and there was a crown given him, and he went forth conquering that he might conquer.
\v 3 And when he had opened the second seal, I heard the second beast, saying: Come, & see.
\v 4 And there went forth another horse, red: and he that sat thereon, to him it was given that he should take peace from the earth, and that they should kill one another, and a great sword was given to him.
\v 5 And when he had opened the third seal, I heard the third beast, saying: Come, and see. And behold a black horse, and he that sat upon him, had a balance in his hand.
\v 6 And I heard as it were a voice in the midst of the four beasts saying: Two pounds of wheat for a penny, and thrice two pounds of barley for a penny, and wine and oil hurt thou not.
\v 7 And when he had opened the fourth seal, I heard a voice of the fourth beast, saying: Come, & see.
\v 8 And behold a pale horse: and he that sat upon him, his name was death, and hell followed him. And power was given to him over the four parts of the earth, to kill with sword, with famine, and with death, and with beasts of the earth.
\v 9 And when he had opened the fifth seal: I saw under the altar the souls of them that were slain for the word of God, and for the testimony which they had. \ef - \fr 6.9 \fq Under the altar. \ft Christ as man (no doubt) is this altar, under which the souls of all Martyrs live in heaven, expecting their bodies, as Christ their head hath his body there already. And for correspondence to their place or state in heaven, the Church layeth commonly their bodies also or relics near or under the altars, ¹ where our Saviour's body is offered in the holy Mass: and hath a special provision that no altars be erected or consecrated without some part of a Saint's body or relics. \+it Conc. African. can. 50. Carthag. 5. can. 14.\+it* See S. Hierom \+it cont. Vigilant c. 3.\+it* S. Augustine \+it de civit. li. 8. c. 27.\+it* S. Gregory \+it li. 5. ep. 50. li. 1. ep. 52. li. 2. ep. 58.\+it* Whereunto the Prophet seemeth here to allude, making their souls also to have their being in heaven, as it were under the altar. But for this purpose note well the words of S. Augustine (or what other ancient writer soever was the author thereof) \+it Ser. 11 de Sanctis. Under the altar (saith he) of God I saw the souls of the slain. What is more reverent or honourable, than to rest under the altar on which sacrifice is done to God, and in which our Lord is the Priest: as it is written, Thou art a Priest according to the order of Melchisedec? Rightly do the souls of the just rest under the altar, because upon the altar our Lord's body is offered, neither without cause do the just there call for revenge of their blood, where also the blood of Christ is shed for sinners\+it*, and many other goodly words to that purpose. This place also the wicked heretic Vigilantius (as S. Hierom writing against him witnesseth c. 2) ² abused to prove, that the souls of Martyrs and other Saints were included in some certain place, that they could not be present at their bodies and monuments (where Christian people used in the primitive Church to pray unto them, as Catholic men do yet) nor be where they list, or where men pray unto them. To which the holy doctor answereth at large, that they be wheresoever Christ is according to his humanity: for under that altar they be. Part of his words be these, that you may see how this blessed father refuted in that Heretic the Calvinists so long before they were born. ³ \+it Doest thou\+it* (saith he) \+it prescribe laws to God? Doest thou fetter the Apostles, that they may be kept in prison till the day of judgement, and be kept from their Lord, of whom it is written, They follow the Lamb whither soever he goeth.\+it* ⁴ \+it If the Lamb be in every place, then they that be with the Lamb, must be every-where. And if the devil and wicked spirits gadding abroad in the world with passing celerity, be present every-where: shall holy Martyrs after the shedding of their blood, be kept close under an altar, that they cannot stir out from thence?\+it* So answereth this learned doctor. \fq ¹ \ft Consecration of altars with Saints' relics. \fq ² \ft Saints be present at their tombs and relics. \fq ³ \ft The Calvinists' heresy concerning the Saints confuted by S. Hierom long ago. \fq ⁴ \ft \+it Apoc. 14.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 6.9 \ft Which misliketh our Calvinists so much, that they charge him of great error, in that he saith, Christ according to his humanity is every-where, as though he were an Ubiquitary Protestant. ¹ Where, if they had any judgement, they might perceive that he meaneth not, that Christ or his Saints should be personally present at once in every place alike, as God is: but that their motion, speed, and agility to be where they list, is in comparable, and that their power and operation is accordingly. Which they may learn to be the holy doctor's meaning, ² by the words that follow of the Devil and his ministers: whom he affirmeth to be every-where no otherwise but by their exceeding celerity of being and working mischief now in one place, now in another, and that in a moment. For though they be spirits, yet are they not every-where at once according to their essence. And for our new Divines it were a hard thing to determine, how long Satan (that told our Lord he had circuited the earth) ³ was in his journey, and in the particular consideration and tentation of Job: and how many men he assaulted in that his once circuit. No, no, such curious companions know nothing, nor believe nothing, but that they see with corporal eyes, and teach nothing but the way to infidelity. \fq ¹ \ft They unlearnedly accuse S. Hierom as an Ubiquist. \fq ² \ft How S. Hierom saith, Christ & his Saints are every-where. \fq ³ \ft \+it Iob. 1.\+it*\ef*
\v 10 And they cried with a loud voice, saying: How long Lord (holy and true), judgest thou not and revengest thou not our blood of them that dwell on the earth? \ef - \fr 6.10 \fq And they cried. \ft S. Hierom also against the said Vigilantius reporteth, that he used an argument against the prayers of Saints out of this place, for that these Martyrs cried for revenge, ¹ and could not obtain. But we will report his words, that you may see how like one heretic is to another, these of our days to those of old. \+it Thou sayest in thy book\+it* (saith S. Hierom c. 3.) \+it that whiles we be alive, one of us may pray for another: but after we be dead, no man's prayer shall be heard for another: specially seeing the Martyrs asking revenge of their blood, could not obtain.\+it* So saith the Heretic. Against which the holy Doctor maketh a long refutation, proving that they pray much more after they be in heaven, than they did here in earth: and that they shall be much sooner heard of God, than when they were in the world. But for the Heretic's argument framed out of these words of the Apocalypse thus, \+it These Martyrs did not obtain, ergo Saints do not pray for us\+it*: it was so frivolous, and the antecedent so manifestly false, that he vouchsaved not to stand about it. For it is plain that the Martyrs here were heard, and that their petition should be fulfilled in time appointed by God (whereunto they did and do always conform themselves:) for it was said unto them, \+it That they should rest yet a little time, till &c.\+it* And that Martyrs' prayers be heard in this case, our Saviour testifieth Luc 18 saying, \+it And will not God revenge his elect that cry to him day and night? I say to you, he will quickly revenge them.\+it* And if God do not hear the Saints sometime nor grant their requests, is it therefore consequent that they do not or man not pray? Then Christ himself should not have prayed his father to remove the bitter cup of death from him, because that petition was not granted. \fq ¹ \ft That Saints pray for us, S. Hierom proveth against the Heretic Vigilantius.\ef* \ef - \fr 6.10 \fq Revengest thou not? \ft ¹ They do not desire revenge upon their enemies for hatred, but of charity and zeal of God's honour, praying that his enemies and the persecutors of his Church and Saints, that will not repent, may be confounded: and that our Lord would accelerate his general judgement, that so they might attain the perfect crown of glory promised unto them, both in body and soul: which is to desire the resurrection of their bodies, which then shall triumph perfectly and fully over the persecutors that so cruelly handled the bodies of the elect, which shall then appear glorious to the enemies' confusion. \fq ¹ \ft How Martyrs cry for revenge.\ef*
\v 11 And white stoles were given, to every one of them \f 1 \fr 6.11 \ft This one stole signifieth the glory or bliss of the soul only, but at the day of judgement they shall have it doubled by adding the glory of their body also.\f* one; and it was said to them, that they should rest yet a little time, till their fellow-servants be complete, and their brethren, that are to be slain even as they. \ef - \fr 6.11 \fq Till their fellow servants be complete. \ft There is a certain number that God hath ordained to die for the testimony of truth and the Catholic faith, for conformity of the members of the head \+sc Christ\+sc* our chief Martyr. And till that number be accomplished, the general condemnation of the wicked persecutors shall not come, nor the general reward of the elect.\ef*
\v 12 And I saw, when he had opened the sixth seal, and \f 1 \fr 6.12 \ft c The tribulation that shall fall in the time of Antichrist.\f* behold there was made a great earth-quake, and the sun became black as it were sack-cloth of hair: and the whole moon became as blood:
\v 13 and the stars from heaven fell upon the earth, as the fig-tree casteth her green figs when it is shaken of a great wind:
\v 14 and heaven departed as a book folded together: and every hill, and Islands were moved out of their places.
\v 15 And the kings of the earth, and princes, and tribunes, and the rich, and the strong, and every bond-man, and free-man hid themselves in the dens and the rocks of mountains.
\v 16 And they say to the mountains and the rocks: \x - \xt Osee. 10. Lu. 23, 50.\x* Fall upon us, and hide us from the face of him that sitteth upon the throne, and from the wrath of the Lamb:
\v 17 because the great day of their wrath is come, and who shall be able to stand?
\c 7
\cd The earth being to be punished, 3. they are commanded to save them that are signed in their foreheads: 4. which are described and numbered both of the Jews and Gentiles, blessing God. 13. Of them that were clothed in white stoles or long robes.
\p
\v 1 \sc After\sc* these things I saw four Angels standing upon the four corners of the earth, holding the four winds of the earth, that they should not blow upon the land, nor upon the sea, nor on any tree.
\v 2 \f 1 \fr 7.2 \ft b The Epistle upon All Hallows day.\f* And I saw another Angel ascending from the rising of the sun, having the sign of the living God; & he cried with a loud voice to the four Angels, to whom it was given to hurt the earth and the sea,
\v 3 saying: Hurt not the earth and the sea, nor the trees, till \f 1 \fr 7.3 \ft It is an allusion to the sign of the Cross which the faithful bear in their foreheads, to shew they be not ashamed of Christ. \+it S. August. tract. 43. in lo.\+it*\f* we sign the servants of our God in their foreheads.
\v 4 And I heard the number of them that were signed, an hundred forty four thousand were signed, \f 1 \fr 7.4 \ft c Of all the tribes put together, so many, 144000.\f* of every tribe of the children of Israel.
\v 5 Of the tribe of Juda, twelve thousand signed. Of the tribe of Ruben, twelve thousand signed. Of the tribe of Gad, twelve thousand signed. \f 1 \fr 7.5 \ft He signifieth by these thousands and the multitude following, all the elect: but the elect of the Jews, to be in a certain number: the elect of the Gentiles to be innumerable.\f*
\v 6 Of the tribe of Aser, twelve thousand signed. Of the tribe of Nephthali, twelve thousand signed. Of the tribe of Manasses, twelve thousand signed.
\v 7 Of the tribe of Simeon, twelve thousand signed. Of the tribe of Levi, twelve thousand signed. Of the tribe of Issachar, twelve thousand signed.
\v 8 Of the tribe of Zabulon, twelve thousand signed. Of the tribe of Joseph, twelve thousand signed. Of the tribe of Benjamin, twelve thousand signed.
\v 9 After these things I saw \f 1 \fr 7.9 \ft c The elect of the Gentiles.\f* a great multitude which no man could number, of all nations, and tribes, and peoples, & tongues: standing before the throne, and in the sight of the Lamb, clothed in white robes, and \f 2 \fr 7.9 \ft Boughs of the palm tree be tokens of triumph and victory.\f* palms in their hands:
\v 10 And they cried with a loud voice, saying: Salvation to our God which sitteth upon the throne, and to the Lamb.
\v 11 And all the Angels stood in the circuit of the throne, and of the Seniors, and of the four beasts: and they fell in the sight of the throne upon their faces, and adored God,
\v 12 saying, Amen. Benediction, and glory, and wisdom, and thanks-giving, honour and power and strength to our God for ever and ever. Amen.
\v 13 \f 1 \fr 7.13 \ft The Epistle for many Martyrs.\f* And one of the Seniors answered, and said to me: These that are clothed in the white robes, who be they? and whence came they?
\v 14 And I said to him: My Lord thou knowest. And he said to me: These are they which are come out of great tribulation, and have washed their robes, and made them white in the blood of the Lamb.
\v 15 \f 1 \fr 7.15 \ft The glory of Martyrs.\f* Therefore they are before the throne of God, and they serve him day and night in his temple: and he that sitteth in the throne, shall dwell over them.
\v 16 \x - \xt Esa. 49, 10.\x* They shall no more hunger nor thirst, neither shall the sun fall upon them, nor any heat.
\v 17 Because the Lamb which is in the midst of the throne, shall rule them, and shall conduct them to the living fountains of waters, and \x - \xt Esa. 25, 8. Apo. 21, 4.\x* God will wipe away all tears from their eyes.
\c 8
\cd 1. The seventh seal being opened, there appear Angels with trumpets: 5. and when another Angel poured out fire taken from the altar, upon the earth, there follow diverse tempests. 7. In like manner, whiles four Angels of the seven sound their trumpets, there fell sundry plagues.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \f 1 \fr 8.1 \ft THE 4. VISION.\f* when he had opened the seventh seal, there was made silence in heaven, as it were half an hour.
\v 2 And I saw seven Angels standing in the sight of God: and there were given to them seven trumpets.
\v 3 And another Angel came, and stood \f 1 \fr 8.3 \ft The Priest standing at the altar praying & offering for the people in the time of the high mysteries, Christ himself also being present upon the altar, is a figure of this thing, & thereunto he alludeth.\f* before the altar, having a golden censer: and there were given to him many incenses that he should give of the prayers of all saints upon the altar of gold, which is before the throne of God.
\v 4 And the smoke of the incenses \f 1 \fr 8.4 \ft c If this be S. Michael or any Angel, and not Christ himself, as some take it, Angels offer up the prayers of the faithful, as the 24 Elders did chap. 5. For this word, \+it Saints\+it*, is taken here for the holy persons on earth, as often in the Scripture: though it be not against the Scriptures, that the inferior Saint or Angel in heaven should offer their prayers to God by their superiors there. But hereby we conclude against the Protestants, that it derogateth not from Christ, that Angels or Saints offer our prayers to God, as also is plain of Raphael \+it Tob. 12, 12.\+it*\f* of the prayers of the saints ascended from the hand of the Angel before God.
\v 5 And the Angel took the censer, & filled it of the fire of the altar, and cast it on the earth, and there were made thunders & voices and lightnings, and a great earth-quake.
\v 6 And the seven Angels which had the seven trumpets, prepared themselves to sound with the trumpet.
\v 7 And the first Angel sounded with the trumpet, and there was made hail and fire, mingled in blood, and it was cast on the earth, and the third part of the earth was burnt, & the third part of trees was burnt, and all green grass was burnt.
\v 8 And the second Angel sounded with the trumpet: and as it were a great mountain burning with fire, was cast into the sea, & the third part of the sea was made blood:
\v 9 and the third part of those creatures died, which had lives in the sea, and the third part of the ships perished.
\v 10 And the third Angel sounded with the trumpet, and a great star fell from heaven, burning as it were a torch, and it fell on the third part of the floods, and on the fountains of waters:
\v 11 and the name of the star is called worm-wood. And the third part of the waters was made into worm-wood: and many men died of the waters, because they were made bitter.
\v 12 And the fourth Angel sounded with the trumpet, and the third part of the sun was smitten, and the third part of the moon, and the third part of the stars, so that the third part of them was darkened, and of the day there shined not the third part, and of the night in like manner,
\v 13 And I looked, and heard the voice of one eagle flying through the midst of heaven, saying with a loud voice: Woe, woe, woe to the inhabiters on the earth: because of the rest of the voices of the three Angels which were to sound with the trumpet.
\c 9
\cd The fifth Angel sounding the trumpet, a star falleth. 3. The issuing forth of locusts from the smoke of the deep pit to vex men, 7. and the description of them. 13. The sixth Angel sounding, four Angels are let loose, 18. which with a great troop of horsemen do murder the third part of men.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \f 1 \fr 9.1 \ft Most understand all this of Heretics.\f* the fifth Angel sounded with the trumpet, & I saw \f 2 \fr 9.1 \ft The fall of an Arch heretic, as Arius, Luther, Calvin, out of the Church of God. Which have the key of Hell to open & bring forth all the old condemned heresies buried before in the depth.\f* a star to have fallen from heaven upon the earth, and there was given to him the key of the pit of bottomless depth.
\v 2 And he opened the pit of the bottomless depth: and the smoke of the pit ascended, as the smoke of a great furnace: and the sun was darkened and the air with the smoke of the pit.
\v 3 And from the smoke of the pit there issued forth \f 1 \fr 9.3 \ft c Innumerable petty heretics following their Masters after the opening & the smoke of the bottomless pit.\f* locusts into the earth. And power was given to them, as the scorpions of the earth have power:
\v 4 and it was commanded them that they should not hurt the grass of the earth nor any green thing, nor any tree: but only men which have not the sign of God in their foreheads. \ef - \fr 9.4 \fq Nor any green thing. \ft The Heretics never hurt or seduce the green tree, that is, such as have a living faith working by charity, but commonly they corrupt him in faith who should otherwise have perished for ill life, ¹ and him that is reprobate, that hath neither the sign of the Cross (which is God's mark) in the forehead of his body, nor the note of election in his soul. \fq ¹ \ft Who are seduced by Heretics.\ef*
\v 5 And it was given unto them that they should not kill them: but that they should be tormented five months: and their torments as the torments of a scorpion when he striketh a man.
\v 6 And \x - \xt Apoc. 6, 16.\x* in those days men shall seek for death, and shall not find it: and they shall desire to die, & death shall fly from them.
\v 7 And the similitudes of the locusts, like to horses prepared into battle: and upon their heads as it were crowns like to gold: and their faces as the faces of men. \ef - \fr 9.7 \fq Prepared into battle. \ft Heretics being ever ready to contend, do pretend victory, and counterfeit gold: ¹ in shape as men, as smooth and delicate as women, their tongues and pens full of gall and venin: their hearts obdurate: full of noise and shuffling: their doctrine as pestiferous and full of poison, as the tail and sting of a scorpion, but they endure for a little season. \fq ¹ \ft The manifold hypocrisy of Heretics.\ef*
\v 8 And they had hair as the hair of women; & their teeth were as of lion's.
\v 9 And they had \f 1 \fr 9.9 \ft \+it c coats of mail, or other coats of fence.\+it*\f* habergeons as habergeons of iron, and the voice of their wings as the voice of the chariots of many horses running into battle.
\v 10 And they had tails like to scorpions, and stings were in their tails: and their power was to hurt men five months.
\v 11 \f 1 \fr 9.11 \ft The chief Master of heretics.\f* And they had over them a king, the Angel of the bottomless depth, whose name in Hebrew is \it Abaddon,\it* and in Greek \it Apollyon:\it* in Latin having the name \it Exterminans.\it* \f 2 \fr 9.11 \ft \+it אבדוןἀπολλύων,\+it* In English, \+it Destroyer.\+it*\f*
\v 12 One woe is gone, & behold two woes come yet after these.
\v 13 And the sixth Angel sounded with the trumpet: and I heard one voice from the four horns of the golden altar, which is before the eyes of God,
\v 14 saying to the sixth Angel which had the trumpet: Loose the four Angels which are bound in the great river Euphrates.
\v 15 And the four Angels were loosed, who were prepared for an hour, & a day, and a month, and a year: that they might kill the third part of men.
\v 16 And the number of the army of horse-men was twenty thousand times ten thousand. And I heard the number of them.
\v 17 And so I saw the horses in the vision: and they that sat upon them, had habergeons of fire and of hyacinth and brimstone, and the heads of the horses were as it were the heads of lions: and from their mouth proceedeth fire, and smoke, and brimstone.
\v 18 And by these three plagues was slain the third part of men, of the fire and of the smoke and of the brimstone, which proceeded from their mouth.
\v 19 For the power of the horses is in their mouth, and in their tails. For, their tails be like to serpents, having heads: and in these they hurt.
\v 20 \f 1 \fr 9.20 \ft c Pagans, Infidels, and sinful impenitent Catholics must be condemned also.\f* And the rest of men which were not slain with these plagues, neither \f 2 \fr 9.20 \ft This phrase being the like both in Greek and Latin, signifieth such sorrowful & penal repentance as causeth a man to forsake his former sins and depart from them. \+it ὀυ μετενόησαν ἐκ τῶν ἔργων.\+it* See the same phrase. \+it c. 2, 21. 22. & Act. 8. v. 22.\+it*\f* have done penance from the works of their hands, not to adore devils and idols of gold and silver and brass and stone and wood, which neither can see, nor hear, nor walk, \ef - \fr 9.20 \fq Idols of gold. \ft Here again the new Translators abuse the people, for \+it idols\+it* saying \+it images\+it*: ¹ the place being plainly against the portraits of the Heathen Gods, which are here and in the Psalm 95 called, \+it dæmonia, Devils.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Heret. translation.\ef*
\v 21 and have not done penance from their murders, nor from their sorceries, nor from their fornication, nor from their thefts.
\c 10
\cd Another strong Angel crying out, 3. seven thunders do speak. 6. The Angel sweareth that there shall be time no more, but at the voice of the seventh Angel the mystery shall be fully accomplished. 9. He giveth John a book to devour.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \f 1 \fr 10.1 \ft CHRIST the valiant Angel is here described.\f* I saw another Angel, strong, descending from heaven, clothed with a cloud, and a rain-bow on his head, and his face was as the sun, and his feet as \f 2 \fr 10.1 \fq a pillar \ft \+it pillars\+it*\f* a pillar of fire.
\v 2 And he had in his hand a little book opened: and he put his right foot upon the sea, and his left upon the land.
\v 3 And he cried with a loud voice, as when a lion roareth. And when he had cried, the seven thunders spake their voices.
\v 4 And when the seven thunders had spoken their voices, I was about to write: and I heard a voice from heaven saying to me: Sign the things which the seven thunders have spoken; and \f 1 \fr 10.4 \ft Many great mysteries and truths are to be preserved in the Church, which for causes know to God's providence are not to be written in the book of holy Scripture.\f* write them not.
\v 5 And \x - \xt Dan. 12, 7.\x* the Angel which I saw standing upon the sea and upon the land, \f 2 \fr 10.5 \ft c This was the manner of taking an oath by the true God, as Deut. 32.\f* lifted up his hand to heaven,
\v 6 and he sware by him that liveth for ever and ever, that created heaven and those things which are in it: and the earth, and those things which are in it: and the sea, and those things which are in it: That there shall be time no more:
\v 7 but in the days of the voice of the seventh Angel, when the trumpet shall begin to sound, the mystery of God shall be consummate, as he hath evangelized by his servants the Prophets.
\v 8 And I heard a voice from heaven again speaking with me, and saying: Go, and take the book that is opened, of the hand of the Angel standing upon the sea and upon the land.
\v 9 And I went to the Angel, saying unto him, that he should give me the book. And he said to me: \x - \xt Ezec. 3, 1.\x* Take the book, and \f 2 \fr 10.9 \ft c By earnest study and meditation.\f* devour it: and it shall make thy belly to be bitter, but in thy mouth it shall be \f 3 \fr 10.9 \ft Sweet in the reading, but in fulfilling, somewhat bitter, because it commandeth works of penance and suffering of tribulations.\f* sweet as it were honey.
\v 10 And I took the book of the hand of the Angel, and devoured it: and it was in my mouth as it were honey, sweet. And when I had devoured it, my belly was made bitter,
\v 11 and he said to me: Thou must again prophesy to Nations, and peoples, and tongues, and many kings.
\c 11
\cd S. John measuring the Temple, 3. heareth of two witnesses that shall preach: 7. whom the beast coming up from the sea shall kill. 11. but they rising again ascend into heaven, 13. and seven thousand persons are slain with an earthquake: 15. and at the sound of the seventh Angel, the four and twenty seniors give praise and thanks to God.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* there was given me a reed like unto a rod: and it was said to me: Arise, and measure the Temple of God, and the altar, and them that adore in it.
\v 2 But the court which is without the Temple, cast forth, & measure not that: because it is given to the Gentiles, and they shall tread under-foot the holy city \f 1 \fr 11.2 \ft Three years and a half. Which is the time of Antichrist's reign and persecution.\f* two & forty months:
\v 3 and I will give to my two witnesses, and they shall prophesy a thousand two hundred sixty days, clothed with sack-cloths. \ef - \fr 11.3 \fq My two witnesses. \ft Enoch and Elias, as it is commonly expounded. For, that Elias shall come again before the later day, ¹ \+it it is a most notorious known thing\+it* (to use S. Augustine's words) \+it in the mouths and hearts of faithful men. See li. 20 de Civit. Dei c. 29. Tract. 4 in Joan.\+it* and both of Enoch and Elias, \+it lib. 1 de pec. merit. c. 3.\+it* So the rest of the Latin Doctors. as, S. Hierom \+it ad Pammach. ep. 61 c. 11. & in Psal. 20.\+it* S. Ambrose \+it in Psal. 45.\+it* S. Hilary \+it 20 can. in Mat.\+it* Prosper \+it li. ultimo de Promissionibus c. 13.\+it* S. Gregory \+it li. 14 Moral. c. 11. & ho. 12 in Ezech.\+it* Beda \+it in 9 Marci.\+it* The Greek fathers also, as S. Chrysostom \+it ho. 58 in Mat. & ho. 4 in 2 Thessal. & ho. 21. in Genes. & ho. 22 in ep. ad Hebr.\+it* Theophylacte and Oecumenius \+it in 17 Matthæi.\+it* S. Damascene \+it li. 4 de Orthodoxa fide c. 27.\+it* Furthermore, that they live also in Paradise, it is partly gathered out of the Scripture Ecclici 44, 16. where it is plainly said of Enoch, that \+it he is translated into paradise\+it*, as all our Latin exemplars do read: and of Elias, that he was taken up alive, it is evident \+it 4 Reg. 2.\+it* And S. Irenæus saith, it is the tradition of the Apostles, that they be both there. \+it li. 5 in initio. Dicunt Presbyteri\+it* (saith he) \+it qui sunt Apostolorum Discipuli. So say the Priests or Ancients that are the scholars of the Apostles.\+it* See S. Justine \+it q. 85 ad orthodoxos\+it*. Finally, that they shall return into the company of men in the end of the world, to preach against Antichrist, and to invite both Jews and Gentiles to penance, and so be martyred, as this place of the Apocalypse seemeth plain, so we have in part other testimonies hereof, \+it Malac. 4. Ecclci 44, 16. 48, 10. Matt. 17, 11.\+it* See also Hyppolytus' book \+it of Antichrist and the end of the world.\+it* All which being well considered, the Heretics are too contentious and incredulous, to discredit the same, as they commonly do. \fq ¹ \ft Enoch & Elias yet alive, shall preach in the time of Antichrist.\ef*
\v 4 These are the two olive trees & the two Candlesticks that stand in the sight of the Lord of the earth.
\v 5 And if any man will hurt them, fire shall come forth out of their mouths, and shall devour their enemies. And if any man will hurt them, so must he be slain.
\v 6 These have power to shut heaven, that it rain not in the days of their prophecy: and they have power over the waters to turn them into blood, and to strike the earth with all plague as often as they will.
\v 7 And when they shall have finished their testimony, the \f 1 \fr 11.7 \ft The great Antichrist.\f* beast which ascended from the depth, shall make war against them, and shall overcome them, and kill them.
\v 8 And their bodies shall lie in the streets of the \f 1 \fr 11.8 \ft c He meaneth Hierusalem, named Sodom and Ægypt for the imitation of them in wickedness. So that we see his chief reign shall be there, though his tyranny may extend to all places of the world.\f* great city, which is called spiritually Sodom and Ægypt, where their Lord also was crucified.
\v 9 And there shall of tribes, and peoples, and tongues, and Gentiles, see their bodies for three days, and a half: and they shall not suffer their bodies to be laid in monuments.
\v 10 And the inhabitants of the earth \f 1 \fr 11.10 \ft The wicked rejoice, when holy men are executed by the tyrants of the world, because their life and doctrine are burdenous unto them.\f* shall be glad upon them, and make merry: and shall send gifts one to another, because these two Prophets tormented them that dwelt upon the earth.
\v 11 And after three days and a half, the spirit of life from God entered into them. And they stood upon their feet, and great fear fell upon them that saw them.
\v 12 And they heard a loud voice from heaven saying to them: Come up hither. And they went up into heaven in a cloud: and their enemies saw them.
\v 13 And in that hour there was made a great earth-quake: and the tenth part of the city fell: and there were slain in the earth-quake names of men seven thousand: and the rest were cast into a fear, and gave glory to the God of heaven.
\v 14 The second woe is gone: and behold the third woe will come quickly.
\v 15 And the seventh Angel sounded with a trumpet: and there were made loud voices in heaven saying, \f 1 \fr 11.15 \ft The kingdom of this world usurped before by Satan & Antichrist, shall afterward be Christ's forever.\f* The kingdom of this world is made our Lord's & his Christ's, and he shall reign for ever and ever. Amen.
\v 16 And the four and twenty Seniors which sit on their seats in the sight of God, fell on their faces, and adored God,
\v 17 saying: We thank thee Lord God omnipotent, which art, and which wast, and which shalt come: because thou hast received thy great power, and hast reigned.
\v 18 And the Gentiles were angry, and thy wrath is come, and the time of the dead, to be judged, and \f 1 \fr 11.18 \ft To repay the hire or wages (for so both the Greek word and the Latin signify due to holy men, proveth against the Protestants, that they did truly merit the same in this life.\f* to render reward to thy Servants the Prophets and Saints, and to them that fear thy name, little and great, and to destroy them that have corrupted the earth.
\v 19 And the temple of God was opened in heaven: and the ark of his testament was seen in his temple, and there were made lightnings, and voices, and an earth-quake and great hail.
\c 12
\cd 4. The great dragon (the Devil) watching the woman that brought forth a man child, to devour it, God took away the child to himself, and fed the woman in the desert. 7. Michael fighting with the dragon overcometh him, 13. \it who being thrown down to the earth, persecuteth the woman & her seed.\it*
\p
\v 1 And \f 1 \fr 12.1 \ft THE 3. PART. The Dragon's incredulous & persecuting multitude, and Antichrist the chief head thereof.\f* a great sign appeared in heaven; \f 2 \fr 12.1 \ft This is properly & principally spoken of the Church: and by allusion, of our B. Lady also.\f* a woman clothed with the sun, and the moon under her feet, & on her head a crown of twelve stars:
\v 2 and being with child, she cried also travailing, and is in anguish to be delivered.
\v 3 And there was seen another sign in heaven, and behold \f 1 \fr 12.3 \ft c The great Devil Lucifer.\f* a great red dragon having seven heads, & ten horns: and on his heads seven diadems,
\v 4 and his tail drew \f 1 \fr 12.4 \ft The spirits that fall from their first state into Apostasy with him and by his means.\f* the third part of the stars of heaven, and cast them to the earth: and the dragon stood before the woman which was ready to be delivered; that when she should be delivered, he might \f 2 \fr 12.4 \ft c The Devil's endeavour against the Church's children, and specially our B. Lady's only son the head of the rest.\f* devour her son.
\v 5 And she brought forth a man child, who was \x - \xt Ps. 2, 9. Apoc. 2, 27.\x* to govern all nations in an iron rod; & her son was taken up to God and to his throne,
\v 6 & the woman fled into the wilderness where she had a place prepared of God, that there they might feed her a thousand two hundred sixty days. \ef - \fr 12.6 \fq The woman fled. \ft ¹ This great persecution that the Church shall flee from, is in the time of Antichrist, and shall endure but three years and a half, as is noted \+it v. 14\+it* in the margent, In which time for all that, she shall not want our Lord's protection, nor true Pastors, nor be so secret, but all faithful men shall know and follow her: much less shall she decay, err in faith, or degenerate and follow Antichrist, as Heretics do wickedly feign. As the Church Catholic now in England in this time of persecution, because it hath no public register nor open free exercise of holy functions, may be said to be fled into the desert, yet it is neither unknown to the faithful that follow it, nor the enemies that persecute it: as the hid company that the protestants talk of, was for some worlds together, neither known to their friends nor foes, because there was indeed none such for many ages together. And this is true, if we take this flight for a very corporal retiring into wilderness. Where indeed it may be, and is of most expounded, to be a spiritual flight, by forsaking the joys and solaces of the world, and giving herself to contemplation and penance, during the time of persecution under Antichrist. And by enlarging the sense, it may well signify the desolation and affliction that the Church suffereth and hath suffered from time to time in this wilderness of the world, by all the forerunners and ministers of Antichrist, Tyrants and Heretics. \fq ¹ \ft The Church shall flee as to a desert in Antichrist's time, but not decay or be unknown, no not for so short a time.\ef*
\v 7 And there was made a great battle in heaven, Michael and his Angels fought with the dragon, and the dragon fought and his Angels: \ef - \fr 12.7 \fq A great battle. \ft ¹ In the Church there is a perpetual combat between Michael (protector of the Church, as whose times he was sometime of the Synagogue \+it Dan. 10. 20\+it*) and his Angels, and the Devil and his ministers. The perfect victory over whom, shall be at his judgement. Mark here also the cause why S. Michael is commonly painted fighting with a dragon. \fq ¹ \ft S. Michael fighting with the dragon.\ef*
\v 8 and they prevailed not, neither was their place found any more in heaven.
\v 9 And that great dragon was cast forth, the old serpent, which is called the devil and Satan, which seduceth the whole world: and he was cast into the earth, & his Angels were thrown down with him.
\v 10 And I heard a great voice in heaven saying: Now is there made salvation and force, and the Kingdom of our God, and the power of his Christ: because the accuser of our brethren is cast forth, who accused them before the sight of our God day and night.
\v 11 And \f 1 \fr 12.11 \ft When the Angels or we have the victory, we must know it is by the blood of Christ, and so all is referred always to him.\f* they overcame him by the blood of the Lamb, and by the word of their testimony, and they loved not their lives even unto death.
\v 12 Therefore rejoice, o heavens, and you that dwell therein. Woe to the earth & to the sea, because the devil is descended to you, having great wrath, knowing that he hath a little time.
\v 13 And after the dragon saw that he was thrown into the earth, he persecuted the woman which brought forth the man-child:
\v 14 and there were given to the woman two wings of a great eagle, that she might fly into the desert unto her place, where she is nourished for \f 1 \fr 12.14 \ft This often insinuation that Antichrist's reign shall be but three years & a half (Dan. 7, 25. Apoc. 11, 2. 3. & in this chap. v. 6. c. 13, 5. ) proveth that the heretics be exceedingly blinded with malice, that hold the Pope to be Antichrist, who hath ruled so many Ages.\f* a time & times, & half a time, from the face of the serpent.
\v 15 And the serpent cast out of his mouth after the woman, water as it were a flood: that he might make her to be carried away with the flood. \ef - \fr 12.15 \fq To be carried away. \ft ¹ By great persecution he would draw her, that is, her children from the true faith: at whose constancy he being the more offended, worketh malicious attempts in assaulting the frailer sort, who are here signified by \+it the rest of her seed that keep the commandments,\+it* but are not so perfect as the former. \fq ¹ \ft Antichrist's attempts to draw from faith.\ef*
\v 16 And the earth holp the woman, and the earth opened her mouth, and swallowed up the flood which the dragon cast out of his mouth.
\v 17 And the dragon was angry against the woman: and went to make battle with the rest of her seed, which keep the commandments of God, and have the testimony of \sc Jesus\sc* Christ.
\v 18 And he stood upon the sand of the sea.
\c 13
\cd 1. A beast rising up out of the sea, having seven heads and ten horns & ten diadems, 5. blasphemeth God, 7. and warreth against the Saints & destroyeth them. 11. And another beast rising out of the earth with two horns, was altogether for the foresaid beast, constraining men to make and adore the image thereof, and to have the character of his name.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* I saw a beast coming up from the sea, having seven heads, and ten horns, & upon his horns ten diadems, and upon his heads names of blasphemy. \ef - \fr 13.1 \fq A beast coming up. \ft This beast is the universal company of the wicked, whose head is Antichrist: & the same is called \+it (Apoc. 17)\+it* the whore of Babylon. ¹ The 7 heads be expounded \+it (Apoc. 17)\+it* seven kings: five before Christ, one present, and one to come. The 10 horns be also there expounded to be 10 kings that shall reign a short while after Antichrist. This dragon is the Devil, by whose power the whore or beast or Antichrist worketh. For in the words following (v. 3 & 4) Antichrist is called the beast, to whom the dragon, that is, the Devil giveth that power of feigned miracles. And as we adore God for giving power to Christ and his followers, so they shall adore the Devil for assisting Antichrist and giving him power. \fq ¹ \ft Many mysteries expounded.\ef*
\v 2 And the beast which I saw, was like to a libard, and his feet as of a bear, and his mouth, as the mouth of a lion. And the dragon gave him his own force and great power.
\v 3 And I saw one of his heads as it were slain to death: and the wound of his death was cured. And all the earth was \f 1 \fr 13.3 \ft They that now follow the simplest & grossest heretics that ever were without seeing miracles, would then much more follow this great seducer working miracles.\f* in admiration after the beast.
\v 4 And they adored the dragon which gave power to the beast: and they adored the beast, saying: Who is like to the beast? and who shall be able to fight with it?
\v 5 And there was given to it a mouth speaking great things and blasphemies: and power was given to it to work two and forty months.
\v 6 And he opened his mouth unto blasphemies toward God, \f 1 \fr 13.6 \ft No heretics ever liker Antichrist, than these in our days, specially in blasphemies against God's Church, Sacraments, Saints, Ministers, and all sacred things.\f* to blaspheme his name, & his tabernacle, & those that dwell in heaven.
\v 7 And it was given unto him to make battail with the saints, & to overcome them. And power was given him upon every tribe and people, and tongue, and nation, \ef - \fr 13.7 \fq To make battle with the saints. \ft ¹ He shall kill the saints then living, Elias and Enoch, and infinite more that profess Christ. Whereby we must learn, not to marvel when we see the wicked persecute and prevail against the just, in this life. Then shall his great persecution and cruelty try the saint's patience, as his wonderful means to seduce shall try the steadfastness of their faith, ² which is signified by these words following, \+it Here is the patience and the faith of saints.\+it* And when it is said, \+it They adored the beast, whose names are not written in the book of life of the Lamb,\+it* it giveth great solace and hope to all them that shall not yield to such persecutions, that they are of God's elect, and their names written in the book of life. \fq ¹ \ft Great persecution by Antichrist and his ministers. \fq ² \ft Their blessedness that continue constant.\ef*
\v 8 and all that inhabit the earth, adored it, \x - \xt Apoc. 3, 5.\x* whose names be not written in the book of life of the Lamb, which was slain from the beginning of the world.
\v 9 If any man have an ear, let him hear.
\v 10 He that shall lead into captivity, goeth into captivity: \x - \xt Gen. 9, 6. Mt. 26, 52.\x* he that shall kill in the sword, he must be killed with the sword. Here is the patience and the faith of saints.
\v 11 And I saw \f 1 \fr 13.11 \ft Another false prophet inferior to Antichrist, shall work wonders also, but all referred to the honour of his master Antichrist. So doth Calvin & other Arch-heretics pervert the world to the honour of Antichrist, and so do their scholers also for the honour of them.\f* another beast coming up from the earth: and he had two horns, like to a lamb, & he spake as a dragon.
\v 12 And all the power of the former beast he did in his sight: and he made the earth and the inhabitants therein, to adore the first beast, whose wound of death was cured.
\v 13 And he did many signs, so that he made also fire to come down from heaven unto the earth in the sight of men.
\v 14 And he seduceth the inhabitants on the earth through the signs which were given him to do in the sight of the beast, saying to them that dwell on the earth, that they should make the image of the beast which hath the stroke of the sword, and lived. \ef - \fr 13.14 \fq The image of the beast. \ft ¹ They that now refuse to worship Christ's image, would then worship Antichrist's. And we may note here, that as the making or honouring of this image was not against the honour of Antichrist, but wholly for it, as also the image erected of Nabuchodonosor, and the worship thereof was altogether for the honour of him, so is the worship of Christ's image, the honour of Christ himself, and not against him, as Protestants madly imagine. \fq ¹ \ft The honour of Christ's image is for the honour of Christ.\ef*
\v 15 And it was given him to give spirit to the image of the beast, and that the image of the beast should speak: and should make, that whosoever shall not adore the image of the beast, be slain.
\v 16 And he shall make all, little & great, and rich, and poor, and free-men, and bond-men, to have a character in their right hand, or in their foreheads.
\v 17 And that no man may buy or sell, but he that hath the character, or the name of the beast, or the number of his name. \ef - \fr 13.17 \fq The character or the name. \ft As belike for the perverse imitation of Christ, whose image (specially as on the Rood or crucifix) he seeth honoured and exalted in every Church, ¹ he will have his image adored (for that is Antichrist, in emulation of like honour, adversary to Christ) so for that he seeth all true Christian men to bear the badge of his Cross in their foreheads, he likewise will force all his to have another mark, to abolish the sign of Christ. By the like emulation also and wicked opposition he will have his name and the letters thereof to be sacred, and to be worn in men's capes, or written in solemn places, and to be worshipped, as the name of \+sc Jesus\+sc* is and ought to be among Christian men. And as the ineffable name of God was among the Jews expressed by a certain number of 4 characters (therefore called \+it Tetragrammaton\+it* ) ² so it seemeth the Apostle alludeth here to the number of Antichrist's name. And here it is much to be noted, that the Protestants plucking down the image of Christ out of all Churches, ³ & his sign of the cross from men's foreheads, and taking away the honour and reverence of the name \+sc Jesus\+sc*, do make room for Antichrist's image, & mark, and name. And when Christ's image and ensigns or arms shall be abolished, and the Idol of Antichrist set up in stead thereof, as it is already begun: then is the abomination of desolation which was foretold by Daniel and our Saviour. \fq ¹ \ft Antichrist's triple honour against the honour of Christ. \fq ² \ft יהוה \fq ³ \ft The Protestants by abolishing of Christ's image, & cross and irreverence to the name JESUS, make a ready way to the honour of Antichrist.\ef*
\v 18 Here is wisdom. He that hath understanding, let him count the number of the beast. For it is the number of a man; and the number of him is six hundred sixty six. \ef - \fr 13.18 \fq Let him count. \ft Though God would not have it manifest before hand to the world, who in particular this Antichrist should be: yet it pleased him to give such tokens of him, that when he cometh, the faithful may easily take notice of him, according as it is written of the event of other prophecies concerning our Saviour, \+it That when it is come to pass, you may believe\+it*. ¹ ² In the mean time we must take heed that we judge not over rashly of God's secrets, the holy writer here signifying, that it is a point of high understanding, illuminated extraordinarily by God's spirit, to reckon right and decipher truly before hand, Antichrist's name and person. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Io. 14, 29.\+it* \fq ² \ft Antichrist's name secret.\ef* \ef - \fr 13.18 \fq It is the number of a man. \ft A man he must be, and not a Devil or spirit, as here it is clear, and by S. Paul \+it 2 Thessal. 2.\+it* where he is called, \+it the man of sin\+it*. Again, he must be one particular person, ¹ & not a number, a succession, or whole order of any degree of men: because his proper name, and the particular number, and the characters thereof be (though obscurely) insinuated. Which reproveth the wicked vanity of Heretics, that would have Christ's own Vicars, the successors of his chief Apostle, yea the whole order of them for many ages together, to be this Antichrist. Who by his description here and in the said Epistle to the Thessalonians, must be one special man, and of a particular proper name, as our Lord \+sc Jesus\+sc* is. And whosoever he be, these Protestants undoubtedly are his precursors. For as they make his way by ridding away Christ's images, cross, and name, so they exceedingly promote the matter by taking away Christ's chief minister, that all may be plain for Antichrist. \fq ¹ \ft Antichrist shall be one special man, and of a peculiar name.\ef* \ef - \fr 13.18 \ft ¹ If the Pope had been Antichrist, and had been revealed now a good many years sithence, as these fellows say he is to them, then the number of this name would agree to him, and the prophecy being now fulfilled, it would evidently appear that he bare the name and number here noted. For (no doubt) when he cometh, this count of the letters or number of his name which before is so hard to know, will be easy. For he will set up his name in every place, even as we faithful men do not advance \+sc Jesus\+sc*. And what name proper or appellative of all or any of the Popes do they find to agree with this number, notwithstanding they boast that they have found the whole order and every of them these thousand years to be Antichrist, and the rest before even from S. Peter, foreworkers toward his kingdom? \fq ¹ \ft The Pope cannot be Antichrist.\ef* \ef - \fr 13.18 \fq The number 666. \ft Forasmuch as the ancient expositors and other do think (for certain knowledge thereof no mortal man can have without an express revelation) ¹ that his name consisteth of so many, & such letters in Greek, as according to their manner of numbering by the Alphabet make 666, and forasmuch as the letters making that number, may be found in diverse names both proper & common: (as S. Irenæus findeth them in \+it Latinos\+it* and \+it Teitan\+it*, Hyppolytus in \+it άρνοῦμαι\+it*, Aretas in \+it Lampetis\+it*, & some of this age in \+it Luderus\+it*, which was Luther's name in the Alman tongue:) ² therefore we see there can be no certain, and every one frameth and applieth the letters to his own purpose, and most absurd folly it is of the Heretics, to apply the word, \+it Latinos\+it*, to the Pope: neither the whole order in common, nor ever any particular Pope being so called, and S. Irenæus the first that observed it in that word, applied it to the Empire and state of the Roman Emperor, which then was Heathen, and not to the Pope of his days or after him: and yet proferred the word, Teitan, as more agreeable, with this admonition, that it were a very perilous and presumptuous thing to define any certainty before hand, of that number and name. And truly whatsoever the Protestants presume herein of the Pope, we may boldly discharge Luther of that dignity. He is undoubtedly one of Antichrist's precursors, but not Antichrist himself. \fq ¹ \ft All framing of letters to express Antichrist's name, is uncertain. \fq ² \ft \+it Iren. li. 5. in fine.\+it*\ef*
\c 14
\cd 1. Virgins follow the Lamb whithersoever, singing a new canticle. 6. One Angel Evangelizeth the Gospel: 8. another Angel telleth the fall of Babylon: 9. the third declareth their torments that have adored the beast. Moreover two having sicles, 15. one of them is commanded to reap down the corn, 18. the other to gather the grapes as in vintage, which are trodden in the lake of God's wrath.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \f 1 \fr 14.1 \ft The Epistle upon S. Innocents' day in Christmas.\f* I looked, & behold \f 2 \fr 14.1 \ft Christ, and the same number of elect that were signed \+it chap. 7.\+it*\f* a Lamb stood upon mount Sion, & with him an hundred forty four thousand having his name, and the name of his Father written in their foreheads.
\v 2 And I heard a voice from heaven, as the voice of many waters, and as the voice of great thunder: and the voice which I heard, as of harpers harping on their harps.
\v 3 And they sang as it were a new song before the seat and before the four beasts, and the Seniors, & no man could \f 1 \fr 14.3 \fq say \ft \+it learn μαθεῖν,\+it*\f* say the song, but those hundred forty four thousand, that were bought from the earth.
\v 4 These are they which were not defiled with women. For they are \f 1 \fr 14.4 \ft One state of life more excellent than another. And virgins for their purity passing the rest, & always accompanying Christ according to the Church's hymn out of this place. \+it Quocunque pergis, virgines sequuntur &c.\+it*\f* virgins. These follow the Lamb whithersoever he shall go. These were bought from among men, \f 2 \fr 14.4 \ft c This the Church applieth to the holy Innocents that died first for Christ.\f* the first fruits to God and the Lamb:
\v 5 and in their mouth there was found no lie. For they are without spot before the throne of God.
\v 6 And I saw another Angel flying through the midst of heaven, having the eternal Gospel, to evangelize unto them that sit upon the earth, and upon every nation, and tribe, and tongue, and people;
\v 7 saying with a loud voice: Fear our Lord, and give him honour, because the hour of his judgement is come: and adore ye him \x - \xt Ps. 145. Act. 14.\x* that made heaven and earth, the sea and all things that are in them, and the fountains of waters.
\v 8 And another Angel followed, saying: \x - \xt Esa. 21. Ier. 51. Apo. 18.\x* Fallen fallen is that great \f 2 \fr 14.8 \ft The city of the devil, which is the universal society of the wicked misbelievers & ill livers in the world.\f* Babylon, which of the wine of the wrath of her fornication made all nations to drink.
\v 9 And the third Angel followed them, saying with a loud voice: If any man adore the beast and his image, and receive the character in his forehead, or in his hand;
\v 10 \f 1 \fr 14.10 \ft The great damnation that shall follow them that forsake Christ & the Church, & worship Antichrist or his image.\f* he also shall drink of the wine of the wrath of God, which is mingled with pure wine in the cup of his wrath, and shall be tormented with fire & brimstone in the sight of the holy Angels and before the sight of the Lamb.
\v 11 And the smoke of their torments shall ascend for ever and ever: neither have they rest day and night, which have adored the beast, and his image, and if any man take the character of his name.
\v 12 Here is the patience of saints, which \f 1 \fr 14.12 \ft Faith is not enough to salvation, without fulfilling of God's commandments.\f* keep the commandments of God and the faith of \sc Jesus\sc*.
\v 13 \f 1 \fr 14.13 \ft b The Epistle in a daily Mass for the dead.\f* And I heard a voice from heaven, saying to me: Write, Blessed are the dead which die in our Lord. From hence-forth now, saith the Spirit, that they rest from their labours. For their works follow them. \ef - \fr 14.13 \fq From hence forth now. \ft This being specially spoken of Martyrs (as not only S. Augustine seemeth to take it, but the Calvinists themselves, translating, \+it in domino, for our Lord's cause\+it* ) ¹ the Protestants have no reason to use the place against Purgatory or prayer for the departed, seeing the Catholic Church and all her children confess, that all Martyrs are straight after their death in bliss, and need no prayers. ² Whereof this is S. Augustine's known sentence, \+it He doeth injury to the Martyr, that prayeth for the Martyr. Ser. 17 de verb. Apost. c. 1.\+it* and again to this purpose he writeth thus most excellently tract. 84 in Joan. \+it We keep not a memory of Martyrs at our Lord's table, as we do of other that rest in peace, that is, for the intent to pray for them, but rather that they may pray for us &c.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft Beza. \fq ² \ft Praying for the dead, and unto Saints, at the altar.\ef* \ef - \fr 14.13 \ft But if we take the words generally for all deceases in state of grace, as it may be also, then we say that even such, though they be in Purgatory and God's chastisement in the next life, and need our prayers, ¹ yet (according to the foresaid words of S. Augustine) \+it do rest in peace\+it*, being discharged from the labours, afflictions, and persecutions of this world, and (which is more) from the daily dangers of sin and damnation, and put into infallible security of eternal joy with unspeakable comfort of conscience. And such indeed are more happy and blessed than any living, who yet are usually in the Scriptures called blessed, even in the middes of the tribulations of this life. Whereby we see that these words, \+it from hence forth they shall rest from their labours\+it*, may truly agree to them also that are in Purgatory, and so here is nothing proved against Purgatory. Lastly, this adverb, \+it amodo\+it*, in Latin, as in the Greek \+it ἀπάρτι\+it*, ² doth not properly signify, from this present time forward, as though the Apostle had said, that after their death and so forward they are happy: but it noteth and joineth the time past together with the time present, in this sense, that such as have died since Christ's Ascension, when he first entering into heaven opened it for others, go not to \+it Limbus Patrum\+it*, as they were wont before Christ's time, but are in case to go straight to bliss, except the impediment be in themselves. Therefore they are here called blessed, that die now in this state of grace & of the new Testament, in comparison of the old faithful and good persons. \fq ¹ \ft The place abused against Purgatory, answered. \fq ² \ft \+it amodo ἀπάρτι Photius in Lexico.\+it*\ef*
\v 14 And I saw, and behold a white cloud: and upon the cloud one sitting like to the Son of man, having on his head a crown of gold, and in his hand a sharp sickle.
\v 15 And another Angel came forth from the temple, crying with a loud voice to him that sat upon the cloud: \x - \xt Ioel 3. Mat. 13.\x* Thrust in thy sickle, and reap, because the hour is come to reap, for the harvest of the earth is dry.
\v 16 And he that sat upon the cloud, thrust his sickle into the earth, and the earth was reaped.
\v 17 And another Angel came forth from the temple which is in heaven, himself also having a sharp sickle.
\v 18 And another Angel came forth from the altar, which had power over the fire: and he cried with a loud voice to him that had the sharp sickle, saying: Thrust in thy sharp sickle, and gather the clusters of the vineyard of the earth: because the grapes thereof be ripe.
\v 19 And the Angel thrust his sharp sickle into the earth, and gathered the vineyard of the earth, & cast it into the great \f 1 \fr 14.19 \ft \+it c ληνὸν lacum. fat. trough, lake.\+it*\f* press of the wrath of God:
\v 20 and the press was trodden without the city, and blood came forth out of the press, up to the horse bridles, for a thousand six hundred furlongs.
\c 15
\cd 2. They that had now overcome the beast and his image and the number of his name, do glorify God. 6. To seven Angels having the seven last plagues, are given cups full of the wrath of God. 7 \f - \ft THE 4. PART. Of the last plagues & final damnation of the wicked.\f*
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \f 1 \fr 15.1 \ft The tribulations about the day of judgement.\f* I saw another sign in heaven great & marvelous: seven Angels having the seven last plagues. Because in them the wrath of God is consummate.
\v 2 And I saw as it were \f 1 \fr 15.2 \ft c Baptism.\f* a sea of glass mingled with fire, & them that overcame the beast and his image and the number of his name, standing upon the sea of glass, having the harps of God:
\v 3 and singing \f 1 \fr 15.3 \ft The song of Moyses and Christ, is the new Testament and the old.\f* the song of Moyses the servant of God, and the song of the Lamb, saying: Great and marvelous are thy works Lord God omnipotent: just and true are thy ways King of the \f 2 \fr 15.3 \fq worlds \ft \+it Saints\+it*\f* worlds.
\v 4 Who shall not fear thee, o Lord, and magnify thy name? Because thou only art holy, because all nations shall come, and adore in thy sight, because thy judgements be manifest.
\v 5 \f 1 \fr 15.5 \ft THE FIFTH VISION.\f* And after these things I looked, and behold the temple of the tabernacle of testimony was opened in heaven:
\v 6 and there issued forth the seven Angels, having the seven plagues, from the temple: revested with clean and white \f 1 \fr 15.6 \fq stone \ft \+it linen λίνον λίθον.\+it*\f* stone, and girded about the breasts with girdles of gold.
\v 7 And one of the four beasts, gave to the seven Angels seven vials of gold full of the wrath of the God that liveth for ever and ever.
\v 8 And the temple was filled with smoke at the majesty of God, and at his power: and no man could enter into the temple, till the seven plagues of the seven Angels were consummate.
\c 16
\cd Upon the pouring out of the seven cups of God's wrath, on the land, the sea, the fountains, the seat of the beast, Euphrates and the air: there arise sundry plagues in the world.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* I heard a great voice out of the temple, saying to the seven Angels: Go, and pour out the seven vials of the wrath of God upon the earth.
\v 2 And the first went, and poured out his vial upon the earth, & there was made a cruel & very sore wound upon men that had the character of the beast: and upon them that adored the image thereof.
\v 3 And the second Angel poured out his vial upon the sea, and there was made blood as it were of one dead: and every living soul died in the sea.
\v 4 And the third poured out his vial upon the rivers and the fountains of waters: and there was made blood.
\v 5 And I heard the Angel of the waters, saying: Thou art just, o Lord, which art, and which wast, the holy one, because thou hast judged these things:
\v 6 \f 1 \fr 16.6 \ft The great revenge that God will do at the later day upon the persecutors of his Saints.\f* because they have shed the blood of the Saints and Prophets, & thou hast given them blood to drink. For they are worthy.
\v 7 And I heard another, saying: Yea Lord God omnipotent, true and just are thy judgements.
\v 8 And the fourth Angel poured out his vial upon the sun, and it was given unto him to afflict men with heat and fire:
\v 9 and men boiled with great heat, and \f 1 \fr 16.9 \ft The desperate and damned persons shall blaspheme God perpetually. Which shall be only as do not repent in this life.\f* blasphemed the name of God having power over these plagues, neither did they penance to give him glory.
\v 10 And the fifth Angel poured out his vial upon the seat of the beast: and his kingdom was made dark, and they together did eat their tongues for pain:
\v 11 and they blasphemed the God of heaven because of their pains and wounds, and \f 1 \fr 16.11 \ft c See chap. \+it 9. v. 20.\+it* in the margent.\f* did not penance from their works.
\v 12 And the sixth Angel poured out his vial upon that great river Euphrates: and dried up the water thereof that a way might be prepared to the kings from the rising of the sun.
\v 13 And I saw from the mouth \f 1 \fr 16.13 \ft The dragon, is the Devil: the beast, Antichrist, or the society whereof he is head: the false-prophet, either Antichrist himself, or the company of Heretics and seducers that follow him.\f* of the dragon, and from the mouth of the beast, and from the mouth of the false-prophet \f 2 \fr 16.13 \fq three \ft \+it issue forth three\+it*\f* three unclean spirits in manner of frogs.
\v 14 For they are the spirits of devils working signs, and they go forth to the kings of the whole earth to gather them into battle at the great day of the omnipotent God.
\v 15 Behold \x - \xt Apoc. 3.\x* I come as a thief: Blessed is he that watcheth, & keepeth his garments, that he \x - \xt 2. Cor. 5, 3.\x* walk not naked, and they see his turpitude.
\v 16 And he shall gather them into a place which in Hebrew is called \f 1 \fr 16.16 \ft c \+it The hill of sheaves,\+it* by S. Hierom's interpretation.\f* Armagedon.
\v 17 And the seventh Angel poured out his vial upon the air, and there came forth a loud voice out of the temple from the throne, saying: It is done.
\v 18 And there were made lightnings, and voices, and thunders, and a great earth-quake was made, such an one as never hath been since men were upon the earth, such an earth-quake, so great.
\v 19 And \x - \xt Ier. 25, 15.\x* the great city was made into three parts: and the cities of the Gentiles fell. And Babylon the great came into memory before God, \f 2 \fr 16.19 \ft The city or commonwealth of the wicked divided into three parts: into infidels, Heretics, and evil Catholics. This city is here called Babylon, whereof see the Annotat. upon the next chapter \+it v. 5.\+it*\f* to give her the cup of wine of the indignation of his wrath.
\v 20 And every Island fled, and mountains were not found.
\v 21 And great hail like a talent came down from heaven upon men: and men blasphemed God for the plague of the hail: because it was made exceeding great.
\c 17
\cd The harlot Babylon clothed with diverse ornaments, 6. and drunken of the blood of Martyrs, sitteth upon a beast that hath seven heads and ten horns: 7. all which things the Angel expoundeth.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* there came one of the seven Angels which had the seven vials, & spake with me, saying: Come, I will shew thee \f 1 \fr 17.1 \ft The final damnation of the whole company of the reprobate, called here the great whore.\f* the damnation of the great harlot, which sitteth upon \f 2 \fr 17.1 \ft c These many waters are many peoples. \+it v. 15.\+it*\f* many waters.
\v 2 with whom the kings of the earth have fornicated, & they which inhabit the earth have been drunk of the wine of her whoredom.
\v 3 And he took me away in spirit into the desert. And I saw a woman sitting upon a scarlet coloured beast, full of names of blasphemy, having seven heads, and ten horns.
\v 4 And the woman was clothed round about with purple and scarlet, and gilted with gold, and precious stone, and pearls, having a golden cup in her hand, full of the abomination & filthiness of her fornication.
\v 5 And in her forehead a name written, \it Mystery:\it* Babylon the great, mother of the fornications and the abominations of the earth. \ef - \fr 17.5 \fq Babylon. \ft In the end of S. Peter's first Epistle, where the Apostle dateth it at Babylon, which the ancient writers (as we there noted) affirm to be meant of Rome: ¹ the Protestants will not in any wise have it so, because they would not be driven to confess that Peter ever was at Rome. But here for that they think it maketh for their opinion, that the Pope is Antichrist, and Rome the seat and city of Antichrist, they will needs have Rome to be this Babylon, this great whore, and this purple harlot. For such fellows, in the exposition of holy Scripture, be led only by their prejudicate opinions and heresies, to which they draw all things without all indifferency and sincerity. But S. Augustine, Aretas, and other writers, most commonly expound it, neither of Babylon itself a city of Chaldæa or Ægypt, nor of Rome, or any one city, which may be so called spiritually, as Hierusalem before chap. 11 is named spiritual Sodom and Ægypt: ² but of the general society of the impious, & of those that prefer the terrene kingdom and commodity of the world, before God & eternal felicity. The author of the Commentaries upon the Apocalypse set forth in S. Ambrose name, writeth thus: \+it This great whore sometime signifieth Rome, specially which at that time when the Apostle wrote this, did persecute the Church of God, but otherwise it signifieth the whole city of the Devil, that is, the universal corpse of the reprobate.\+it* Tertullian also taketh it for Rome, ³ thus. \+it Babylon\+it* (saith he) \+it in S. John is a figure of the city of Rome, being so great, so proud of the Empire, and the destroyer of the saints.\+it* Which is plainly spoken of that city, when it was heathen, the head of the terrene dominion of the world, the persecutor of the Apostles and their successors, the seat of Nero, Domitian, and the like, Christ's special enemies, the sink of idolatry, sin, and false worship of the Pagan gods. Then was it Babylon, when S. John wrote this, and then was Nero and the rest figures of Antichrist, and that city the resemblance of the principal place (wheresoever it be) that Antichrist shall reign in, about the later end of the world. \fq ¹ \ft The Protestants here will needs have Babylon to be Rome, but not in S. Peter's epistle. \fq ² \ft By Babylon (according to all the Fathers) is signified, partly the whole society of the wicked, partly the city of Rome, only in respect of the terrene & heathenish state of them that persecuted the Church. \fq ³ \ft \+it li. advers. Iudæos.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 17.5 \ft Now to apply that to the Roman Church and Apostolic see, either now or then, which was spoken only of the terrene state of that city, as it was the seat of the Emperor, and not of Peter, when it did slay about 30 Popes Christ's Vicars, one after another, & endeavoured to destroy the whole Church: that is most blasphemous and foolish. ¹ The Church in Rome was one thing, & Babylon in Rome another thing. Peter sat in Rome, and Nero sat in Rome, but Peter, as in the Church of Rome: Nero, as in the Babylon of Rome. Which distinction the Heretics might have learned by S. Peter himself \+it ep. 1. chap. 5.\+it* writing thus, \+it The Church saluteth you, that is in Babylon, co-elect.\+it* So that the Church and the very chosen Church was in Rome, when Rome was Babylon. Whereby it is plain that, whether Babylon or the great whore do here signify Rome or no, yet it cannot signify the Church of Rome: which is now, and ever was, differing from the terrene Empire of the same. And if, as in the beginning of the Church, Nero and the rest of the persecuting Emperors (which were figures of Antichrist) did principally sit in Rome, so also the great Antichrist shall have his seat there, as it may well be (though others think that Hierusalem rather shall be his principal city:) yet even then shall neither the Church of Rome, nor the Pope of Rome be Antichrist, but shall be persecuted by Antichrist, and driven out of Rome, if it be possible. For, to Christ's Vicar and the Roman Church he will bear as much good will as the Protestants now do, and he shall have more power to persecute him and the Church, than they have. \fq ¹ \ft The Church of Rome is never called Babylon.\ef* \ef - \fr 17.5 \ft S. Hierom \+it epist. 17. c. 7.\+it* to Marcella, to draw her out of the city of Rome to the holy land, warning her of the manifold allurements to sin and ill life, that be in so great and populous a city, alludeth at length to these words of the Apocalypse, and maketh it to be Babylon, and the purple whore. But straight way, lest some naughty person might think he meant that of the Church of Rome, which he spake of the society of the wicked only he addeth: \+it There is there indeed the holy Church, there are the triumphant monuments of the Apostles and Martyrs, there is the true confession of Christ, there is the faith praised\+it* ¹ \+it of the Apostle, and Gentility trodden under foot, the name of Christian daily advancing itself on high.\+it* Whereby you see that whatsoever may be spoken or interpreted of Rome, out of this word Babylon, it is not meant of the Church of Rome, but of the terrene state, in so much that the said holy Doctor \+it (li. 2 advers. Iovinian. c. 19.)\+it* signifieth, that the holiness of the Church there, hath wiped away the blasphemy written in the forehead of her former iniquity. But of the difference of the old state and dominion of the Heathen there, for which it is resembled to Antichrist, and the Priestly state which now it hath, read a notable place in S. Leo \+it serm. 1 in natali Petri & Pauli.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft \+it Ro. 1.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 17.5 \fq Mystery. \ft S. Paul calleth this secret and close working of abomination, ¹ the mystery of iniquity. \+it 2. Thessal. 2.\+it* and it is called a little after in this chapter \+it vers. 7. the Sacrament\+it* (or mystery) of the woman, and it is also the mark of reprobation and damnation. \fq ¹ \ft Mystery.\ef*
\v 6 And I saw the woman drunken of the blood of Saints, and of the blood of the Martyrs of \sc Jesus\sc*. And I marveled when I had seen her, with great admiration. \ef - \fr 17.6 \fq Drunken of the blood. \ft It is plain that this woman signifieth the whole corpse of all the persecutors that have and shall shed so much blood of the just: of the Prophets, Apostles, and other Martyrs from the beginning of the world to the end. ¹ The Protestants foolishly expound it of Rome, for that there they put Heretics to death, and allow of their punishment in other countries: but their blood is not called the blood of saints, no more than the blood of thieves man-killers, and other malefactors: for the shedding of which by order of justice, no commonwealth shall answer. ² \fq ¹ \ft This woman signifieth all persecutors of saints. \fq ² \ft Putting heretics to death, is not to shed the blood of saints.\ef*
\v 7 And the Angel said to me: Why doest thou marvel? I will tell thee the mystery of the woman, and of the beast that carrieth her, which hath the seven heads, and the ten horns.
\v 8 The beast which thou sawest, \f 1 \fr 17.8 \ft It signifieth the short reign of Antichrist, who is the chief horn or head of the beast.\f* was, and is not, and shall come up out of the bottomless depth, and go into destruction: and the inhabitants on the earth (whose names are not written in the book of life from the making of the world) shall marvel, seeing the beast that was, and is not.
\v 9 And here is understanding, that hath wisdom. The seven heads, are seven hills, upon which the woman sitteth, and they are seven kings. \ef - \fr 17.9 \fq Seven hills. \ft ¹ The Angel himself here expoundeth these 7 hills to be all one with the 7 heads and the 7 kings: & yet the Heretics blinded exceedingly with malice against the Church of Rome, are so mad to take them for the seven hills literally, upon which in old time Rome did stand: that so they might make the unlearned believe that Rome is the seat of Antichrist. But if they had any consideration, they might mark that the Prophet's visions here are most of them by Seven, whether he talk of heads, horns, candlesticks, Churches, kings, hills, or other things: and that he alluded not to the hills, because they were just seven, but that \+it Seven\+it* is a mystical number, as sometime \+it Ten\+it* is, signifying universally all of that sort whereof he speaketh, as, that the seven heads, hills, or kingdoms (which are here all one) should be all the kingdoms of the world that persecute the Christians: being heads and mountains for their height in dignity above others. And some take it, that there were seven special Empires, kingdoms, or States, that were or shall be the greatest persecutors of God's people, as of Ægypt, Chanaan, Babylon, the Persians, and Greeks, which be five. Sixthly of the Roman Empire which once persecuted most of all other, and which (as the Apostle here saith) \+it yet is\+it*, or standeth. But the seventh, then when S. John wrote this, was not come, neither is yet come in our days: which is Antichrist's state, which shall not come so long as the Empire of Rome standeth, as S. Paul did Prophesy. \+it 2 Thessal. 2.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The Protestants' madness in expounding the 7 hills, of Rome: the Angel himself expounding them otherwise.\ef*
\v 10 Five are fallen, one is, and another is not yet come: and when he shall come, he must tarry a short time.
\v 11 And the beast which was, and is not: the same also is the eight, and is of the seven, & goeth into destruction. \ef - \fr 17.11 \fq The same is the eight. \ft ¹ The beast itself being the congregation of all these wicked persecutors, though it consist of the foresaid seven, yet for that the malice of all is complete in it, may be called the eight. Or. Antichrist himself, though he be one of the seven, yet for his extraordinary wickedness shall be counted the odd persecutor, or the accomplishment of all other, & therefore is named the eight. Some take this beast called the eight, to be the Devil. \fq ¹ \ft What is the eighth beast.\ef*
\v 12 And the ten horns which thou sawest, are \f 1 \fr 17.12 \ft Some expound it of ten small kingdoms, into which the Roman Empire shall be divided, which shall all serve Antichrist both in his life and a little after.\f* ten kings, which have not yet received kingdom, but \f 2 \fr 17.12 \fq shall \ft do\f* shall receive power as kings one hour after the beast.
\v 13 These have one counsel and force: and their power they shall deliver to the beast.
\v 14 These shall fight with the Lamb, and the Lamb shall overcome them, because \x - \xt 1. Tim. 6, 15. Apo. 19, 16.\x* he is Lord of lords, and King of kings, and they that are with him, called, and elect, and faithful.
\v 15 And he said to me: The waters which thou sawest where the harlot sitteth, are peoples, and nations, and tongues.
\v 16 And the ten horns which thou sawest in the beast: these shall hate the harlot, and shall make her desolate and naked, and shall eat her flesh, and her they shall burn with fire.
\v 17 For \f 1 \fr 17.17 \ft Not forcing or moving any to follow Antichrist, but by his just judgement, & for punishment of their sins, permitting them to believe and consent to him.\f* God hath given into their hearts, to do that which pleaseth him: that they give their kingdom to the beast, till the words of God be consummate.
\v 18 And the woman which thou sawest: is the great city, which hath kingdom over the kings of the earth. \ef - \fr 17.18 \fq The great city. \ft If it be meant of any one city, and not of the universal society of the reprobate (which is the city of the Devil, as the Church & the universal fellowship of the faithful is called the city of God) it is most like to be old Rome, as some of the Greek expound it, ¹ from the time of the first Emperors, till Constantine's days, who made an end of the persecution. For by the authority of the old Roman Empire, Christ was put to death first, and afterward the two chief Apostles, & the Popes their successors, and infinite Catholic men throughout the world, by lesser kings which then were subject to Rome. All which Antichristian persecutions ceased, when Constantine reigned, and yielded up the city to the Pope, who holdeth not the kingdom or Empire over the world, as the Heathen did, but the fatherhood and spiritual rule of the Church. Howbeit the more probable sense is the other, of the city of the Devil, as the author of the homilies upon the Apocalypse in S. Augustine, declareth. \fq ¹ \ft The double interpretation of Babylon.\ef*
\c 18
\cd The fall of Babylon, her judgement, plagues and revenges: for the which, 9. the kings, 16. and merchants of the earth that sometime did cleave unto her, shall mourn bitterly: 20. but heaven, and the Apostles and Prophets shall rejoice.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* after these things I saw another Angel coming down from heaven, having great power: and the earth was illuminated of his glory.
\v 2 And he cried out in force, saying: \x - \xt Apo. 14, 8.\x* Fallen fallen is Babylon the great: and it is become the habitation of devils, and \f 2 \fr 18.2 \ft \+it c φυλακὴ\+it*\f* the custody of every unclean spirit, & the custody of every unclean & hateful bird:
\v 3 because all nations have drunk of the wine of the wrath of her fornication: and the kings of the earth have fornicated with her: and the merchants of the earth were made rich by the virtue of her delicacies.
\v 4 And I heard another voice from heaven, saying: Go out from her my people: that you be not partakers of her sins, and receive not of her plagues.
\v 5 Because her sins are come even to heaven, and God hath remembered her iniquities.
\v 6 Render to her as she also hath rendered to you: and double ye double according to her works: In the cup wherein she hath mingled, mingle ye double unto her.
\v 7 As much as she hath glorified herself, & hath been in delicacies, \x - \xt Esa. 47, 8.\x* so much give her torment and mourning: because she saith in her heart, \f 2 \fr 18.7 \ft The measure of pains & damnation, according to the wicked pleasures or unlawful delights of this life. Which is a sore sentence for such people as turn their whole life to lust and riot.\f* I sit a queen, and widow I am not, and mourning I shall not see.
\v 8 Therefore in one day shall her plagues come, death, and mourning, and famine, & with fire she shall be burnt: because God is strong that shall judge her.
\v 9 And \f 1 \fr 18.9 \ft Kings and Merchants are most encumbered, dangered and drowned in the pleasures of this world: whose whole life & traffic is (if they be not exceeding virtuous) to find variety of earthly pleasures. Who seeing once the extreme end of their joys and of all that made their heaven here, to be turned into pains & damnation eternal, then shall howl & weep too late.\f* the kings of the earth, which have fornicated with her, & have lived in delicacies, shall weep, and bewail themselves upon her, when they shall see the smoke of her burning:
\v 10 standing far off for the fear of her torments, saying: Woe, woe, that great city Babylon, that strong city: because in one hour is thy judgement come.
\v 11 And the merchants of the earth shall weep, & mourn upon her: because no man shall buy their merchandise any more,
\v 12 merchandise of gold and silver and precious stone: and of pearl, and fine linen, and purple, and silk, & scarlet and all Thyine wood, and all vessels of ivory, and all vessels of precious stone and of brass and iron and marble,
\v 13 and cinnamon, and of odours, and ointment, and frankincense, and wine, and oil, and flour, & wheat, and beasts, and sheep, and horses, and chariots, & slaves, and souls of men.
\v 14 And the apples of the desire of thy soul are departed from thee, and all fat and goodly things are perished from thee, and they shall no more find them.
\v 15 The merchants of these things which are made rich, shall stand far from her for fear of her torments, weeping and mourning,
\v 16 & saying: Woe, woe, that great city, which was clothed with silk, and purple, and scarlet, and was gilted with gold, and precious stone, and pearls:
\v 17 because in one hour are so great riches made desolate: and every governor, & every one that saileth into the lake, and the ship-men, and they that work in the sea, stood a far off,
\v 18 and cried seeing the place of her burning, saying: What other is like to this great city?
\v 19 And they threw dust upon their heads, and cried weeping and mourning, saying: Woe, woe, that great city, in the which all were made rich that had ships in the sea, of her prices: because in one hour she is desolate.
\v 20 \f 1 \fr 18.20 \ft c The Angels and all Saints shall rejoice and laud God to see the wicked confounded, & God's justice executed upon their oppressors & persecutors, & this is that which the Martyrs prayed for, \+it chap. 6.\+it*\f* Rejoice over her, heaven, and ye holy Apostles and Prophets: because God hath judged your judgement of her.
\v 21 And one strong Angel took up as it were a great millstone, and threw it into the sea, saying: \x - \xt Ier. 51, 63.\x* With this violence shall \f 2 \fr 18.21 \ft By this is seemeth clear that the Apostle meaneth not any one city, but the universal company of the reprobate, which shall perish in the day of judgement. The old prophets also naming the whole number of God's enemies mystically, Babylon as \+it Ierem. c. 52.\+it*\f* Babylon that great city be thrown, and shall now be found no more.
\v 22 And the voice of harpers, and of Musicians, and of them that sing on shalm and trumpet, shall no more be heard in thee, & every artificer of every art shall be found no more in thee, and the noise of the mill shall no more be heard in thee,
\v 23 and the light of the lamp shall no more shine in thee, & the voice of the bridegroom and the bride shall no more be heard in thee: because thy merchants were the princes of the earth, because all nations have erred in thine enchantments.
\v 24 And in her is found the blood of the Prophets and Saints, and of all that were slain in the earth.
\c 19
\cd 2. The Saints glorifying God for the judgement pronounced upon the harlot, 7. the marriage of the Lamb is prepared. 10. The Angel refuseth to be adored of S. John. 11. There appeareth one (who is the word of God, and the King of kings and Lord of lords) sitting on a horse, with a great army, and fighting against the beast and the kings of the earth and their armies: 17. the birds of the air being in the mean time called to devour their flesh.
\p
\v 1 \sc After\sc* \f 1 \fr 19.1 \ft The Epistle for many Martyrs, between Easter & Whitsunday.\f* these things I heard as it were the voice of many multitudes in heaven saying, \it Allelu-ia.\it* \f 2 \fr 19.1 \ft ALLELUIA.\f* Praise, and glory and power is to our God:
\v 2 because true & just are his judgements which hath judged of the great harlot, that hath corrupted the earth in her whoredom, and hath revenged the blood of his servants, of her hands.
\v 3 And \f 1 \fr 19.3 \ft This often repeating of \+it Allelu-ia\+it* in times of rejoicing, the Church doth follow in her Service.\f* again they said, \it Allelu-ia.\it* And her smoke ascendeth for ever and ever.
\v 4 And the four and twenty Seniors fell down, and the four beasts, & adored God sitting upon the throne, saying: \it Amen, Allelu-ia.\it* \ef - \fr 19.4 \fq Amen, Alleluia. \ft ¹ These two Hebrew words (as other elsewhere) both in the Greek & Latin text are kept religiously, & not translated, ² unless it be once or twice in the Psalms. Yea and the Protestants themselves keep them in the text of their English Testaments in many places: and marvel it is why they use them not in all places, but sometimes turn, \+it Amen\+it*, into \+it verily\+it*, whereof see the Annotation \+it Joan. 8. v. 34\+it*: and in their Service book they translate, \+it Alleluia\+it*, into, \+it Praise ye the Lord.\+it* as though \+it Alleluia\+it* had not as good a grace in the act of serving God, (where it is indeed properly used) as it hath in the text of the Scripture. \fq ¹ \ft הללויה אמן \fq ² \ft \+it Amen, Alleluia\+it*, not translated.\ef* \ef - \fr 19.4 \ft The Church Catholic doth often and specially use this sacred word, to join with the Church triumphant, consisting of Angels and Saints, who here are said to laud and praise God with great rejoicing, by this word \+it Alleluia\+it*, ¹ and by often repetition thereof: as the Catholic Church also useth, namely in Easter time even till Whitsuntide, for the joy of Christ's resurrection, which (as S. Augustine declareth \+it ep. ad Ianuarium\+it* ) ² was the general use of the Primitive Church, making a greater mystery and matter of it, than our Protestants now do. At other times of the year also he saith it was sung in some Churches, but not in all. And S. Hierom numbereth it among the heresies of Vigilantius, that \+it Alleluia\+it* could not be sung but at Easter. \+it Advers. Vigilant. c. 1.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft \+it Alleluia\+it* often used in the Church, specially in Easter time. \fq ² \ft \+it Epist. ad Ian. c. 17. & c. 15.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 19.4 \ft The truth is, by the use of the Scriptures it hath more in it than, \+it Praise ye the Lord\+it*, ¹ signifying with laud, glorifying, and praising of God, a great rejoicing withal, mirth, and exultation of heart in the singers thereof. And that is the cause why the holy Church saith, \+it Laus tibi Domine, Praise be to thee o Lord\+it*, in Lent and times of penance and mourning, but not \+it Alleluia.\+it* which (as S. Augustine also declareth) is a term of signification and mystery, joined with that time, and then used specially in the Church of God, when she representeth to us in her Service, the joys and beatitude of the next life: which is done specially at Easter, by the joyful celebrating of Christ's glorious Resurrection and Ascension, after the penal time of Lent which representeth the misery of this life. See S. Augustine \+it Ser. 1 & 5. c. 9. & 6. c. 9 de Diversis to. 10.\+it* and his enarration upon the 148 Psalm. For in the titles and ends of diverse holy Psalms this \+it Alleluia\+it* is full of mystery & sacred signification. Where we must ask the Protestants, why they have left it out altogether, being in the Hebrew, saying neither \+it Alleluia\+it*, nor, \+it Praise ye the Lord\+it*, in the Bible 1577: ² & that nine times in the six last Psalms. \fq ¹ \ft It signifieth more than (as the Protestants translate it) \+it praise ye the Lord\+it*. \fq ² \ft False translation.\ef* \ef - \fr 19.4 \ft Moreover the said holy Doctor \+it (li. 2 de doct. Christ. c. 11)\+it* affirmeth that \+it Amen\+it* & \+it Alleluia\+it* be not translated into any other language, ¹ \+it propter sanctiorem authoritatem\+it*, for the more sacred authority of the words so remaining. And \+it ep. 178.\+it* he saith that it is not lawful to translate them. \+it Nam sciendum est &c. for it is to be known\+it* (saith he) \+it that all nations do sing Amen\+it* and \+it Alleluia in the Hebrew words, which neither the Latin man nor the Barbarous may translate into his own language\+it*. See S. Hierom also \+it epist. 137\+it*. And namely for our Nation, S. Gregory will bear us witness that our country received the word \+it Alleluia\+it* with their Christianity, ² saying thus \+it li. 27 Moral. c. 6 Lungua Britannia quæ nihil, aliud nonerat quam barbarum frendere, iamdudum in Divinis laudibus Hebræum coepit resonare Alleluia.\+it* that is, \+it The Britan tongue, which knew nothing else but to mutter barbarously, hath begun of late in God's divine lauds and praises to sound the Hebrew Alleluia\+it*. And for Jury, S. Hierom \+it ep. 17. c. 7\+it* writeth, that the husbandmen at the plough sang \+it Alleluia\+it*, which was not then their vulgar speech. Yea he saith that in Monasteries the singing of \+it Alleluia\+it* was in stead of a bell to call them together \+it ad Collectam. in Epitaph. Paulæ c. 10.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft \+it Amen\+it* and \+it Alleluia\+it* should not be translated into vulgar tongues. \fq ² \ft All nations in the Primitive Church sang \+it Amen\+it* and \+it Alleluia\+it*.\ef* \ef - \fr 19.4 \ft This word is a sacred, Christian, mystical, and Angelical song, and yet in the new service book it is turned into, \+it Praise ye the Lord\+it*, and \+it Alleluia\+it* is quite gone, ¹ because they list neither to agree with the Church of God, nor with the use of holy Scriptures, no nor with their own translations. But no marvel, that they cannot sing \+it the song of our Lord\+it* and of Angels \+it in a strange country\+it*, ² that is, out of the Catholic Church, in the captivity of schism & heresy. Lastly, we might ask them whether it be all one to say \+it Mat. 21, Hosanna\+it*, &, \+it Save us we beseech thee?\+it* Whereas Hosanna is withal a word of exceeding congratulation and joy which they expressed toward our Saviour. Even so \+it Alleluia\+it* hath another manner of sense and signification it it, than can be expressed by, \+it Praise ye the Lord.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The Protestants' profane this word by translating it, and diminish the signification thereof. \fq ² \ft \+it Psal. 136.\+it*\ef*
\v 5 And a voice came out from the throne, saying: Say praise to our God all ye his servants: and you that fear him, little and great.
\v 6 And I heard as it were the voice of a great multitude, and as the voice of many waters, & as the voice of great thunders, saying, \it Allelu-ia:\it* because our Lord God the omnipotent hath reigned.
\v 7 Let us be glad and rejoice, and give glory to him: because \f 1 \fr 19.7 \ft At this day shall the whole Church of the elect be finally & perfectly for ever joined unto Christ in marriage inseparable.\f* the marriage of the Lamb is come, & his wife hath prepared herself.
\v 8 And it was given to her that she clothe herself with silk glittering and white. For the silk are the justifications of Saints. \ef - \fr 19.8 \fq Justifications of Saints. \ft Here the Heretics in their translations could not alter the word \+it justifications\+it* into \+it ordinances\+it*, or \+it constitutions\+it*, as they did falsely in the first of S. Luke, whereof see the Annotation there verse. 4. but they are forced to say in Latin, \+it justificationes\+it* as Beza: and in English, \+it righteousness\+it*, (for \+it justifications\+it* they will not say in any case for fear of inconvenience.) yea and they cannot deny but these justifications be the good works of saints. ¹ But where ² they make this gloss, that they be so called, because they are the fruits or effect of faith and of the justice which we have by only faith, it is most evidently false, & against the very text, and nature of the word. For there is no cause why any thing should be called a man's justification, but for that it maketh him just. So that, \+it justifications\+it*, be the virtues of faith, hope, charity, and good deeds, justifying or making a man just, and not effects of justification. Neither faith only, but they all together be the very ornaments and inward garments, beauty, and justice of the soul, as here it is evident. \fq ¹ \ft Justifications are good works, not as the effects of faith justifying, but because themselves also with faith justify a man. \fq ² \ft Beza.\ef*
\v 9 And he said to me: Write, \x - \xt Mat. 22, Lu. 14,\x* Blessed be they that are called to the \f 2 \fr 19.9 \ft That is the feast of eternal life prepared for his spouse the Church.\f* supper of the marriage of the Lamb. And he said to me: These words of God, be true.
\v 10 And \x - \xt Apoc. 22, 9.\x* I fell before his feet, to adore him. And he saith to me: See thou do not; I am thy fellow-servant, and of thy brethren that have the testimony of \sc Jesus\sc*. Adore God. For the testimony of \sc Jesus\sc*, is the spirit of prophecy. \ef - \fr 19.10 \fq And I fell. \ft The Protestants abuse this place, and the example of the Angel's forbidding John to adore him being but his fellow servant, and appointing him to adore God, against all honour, reverence, and adoration of Angels, Saints, or other sanctified creatures, teaching that no religious worship ought to be done unto them. ¹ But in truth it maketh for no such purpose, but only warneth us that Divine honour and the adoration due to God alone, may not be given to any Angel or other creature. \+it S. August. de vera relig. cap. ultimo.\+it* And when the Adversaries reply that so great an Apostle, as John was, could not be ignorant of that point, nor would have given divine honour unto an Angel (for so he had been an Idolater) and therefore that he was not reprehended for that, but for doing any religious reverence or other honour whatsoever to his fellow-servant: ² we answer that by the like reason, S. John being so great an Apostle, if this later kind of reverence had been unlawful and to be reprehended, as the Protestants hold it is no less than the other, could not have been ignorant thereof, nor would have done it. \fq ¹ \ft S. John's adoring of the Angel, explicated against the Protestants abusing the same. \fq ² \ft The Protestants are refelled by their own reason.\ef* \ef - \fr 19.10 \ft Therefore they might much better have learned of S. Augustine \+it (q. 61 in Genes.)\+it* how this fact of S. John was corrected by the Angel, and wherein the error was. In effect it is thus, That the Angel being so glorious and full of majesty, presenting Christ's person, ¹ and in his name using diverse words proper to God, as, \+it I am the first and the last, and alive and was dead,\+it* ² and such like, might well be taken of S. John, by error of his person, to be Christ himself, and that the Apostle presuming him to be so indeed, adored him with Divine honour: which the Angel correcting, told him he was not God, but one of his fellows, and therefore that he should not so adore him, but God. Thus then we see, John was neither so ignorant, to think that any undue honour might be given to any creature: nor so ill, to commit idolatry by doing undue worship to any Angel in heaven: and therefore was not culpable at all in his fact, but only erred materially (as the Schoolmen call it) that is, by mistaking one for another, thinking that which was an Angel, to have been our Lord: because he knew that our Lord himself is also ³ called an Angel, and hath often appeared in the visions of the faithful. \fq ¹ \ft S. John erred only in the person, mistaking the Angel to be Christ himself, and so adoring him as God. \fq ² \ft \+it Apoc. c. 1.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it Esa. 9. In Græco. Malac. 3.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 19.10 \ft And the like is to be thought of the Angel appearing in the 22 of the Apocalypse, whether it were the same or another. ¹ For that also did so appear, that John could not tell whether it were Christ himself or no, till the Angel told him. Once this is certain, that John did not formally (as they say) commit idolatry, nor sin at all herein, knowing all duties of a Christian man, no less than an Angel of heaven, being also in as great honour with God, yea and in more than many Angels. ² Which perhaps may be the cause (and consequently another explication of this place) that the Angel knowing his great graces and merits before God, would not accept any worship or submission at his hands, though John again of like humility did it, as also immediately afterward chap. 22. which belike he would not have done, if he had been precisely advised by the Angel but a moment before, of error & undutifulness in the fact. Howsoever that be, this is evident, that this the Angels refusing of adoration, taketh not away the due reverence and respect we ought to have to Angels or other sanctified persons and creatures. And so these words, \+it See thou do it not\+it*, signify rather an earnest refusal, than any signification of crime to be committed thereby. And marvel it is that the Protestants making themselves so sure of the true sense of every doubtful place by conference of other Scriptures, follow not here the conference and comparing of Scriptures that themselves so much or only require. ³ We will give them occasion and a method so to do, thus. ⁴ He that doubteth of this place, findeth out three things of question, which must be tried by other Scriptures. The first, whether there ought to be or may be any religious reverence or honour done to any creatures, taking the word religion or religious worship not for that special honour which is properly and only due to God, as S. Augustine sometimes useth it, ⁵ but for reverence due to any thing that is holy by sanctification or application to the service of God. The second thing is, whether by use of Scriptures, that honour be called \+it adoration\+it* in Latin, or by a word equivalent in other languages, Hebrew, Greek, or English. ⁶ Lastly, whether we may by the Scriptures fall down prostrate before the things, or at the feet of the persons that we so adore. For of civil duty done to our Superiors by capping, kneeling, or other courtesy, I think the Protestants will not stand with us: though indeed, their arguments make as much against the one as the other. \fq ¹ \ft S. John sinned not in this adoration. \fq ² \ft Another explication of this place. \fq ³ \ft The Protestants by conference of Scriptures might find religious adoration of creatures. \fq ⁴ \ft Three points herein examined and proved by Scriptures. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Aug. de vera relig. c. 55.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft \+it השתחות προσκυνεῖν.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 19.10 \ft But for religious worship of creatures (which we speak of) let them see in the Scriptures both old and new: ¹ ² first, whether the Temple, the tabernacle, the Ark, the propitiatory, the Cherubins, the altar, the bread of proposition, the Sabboth, and all their holies, were not reverenced by all signs of devotion and religion: whether the Sacraments of Christ, the Priests of our Lord, the Prophets of God, the Gospel, Scriptures, the name of \+sc Jesus\+sc*, such like (which be by use, signification, or sanctification made holy) are not now to be reverenced: and they shall find all these things to have been reverenced of all the faithful, without any dishonour of God, and much to his honour. ³ Secondly, that this reverence is named \+it adoration\+it* in the Scriptures, these speeches do prove \+it Ps. 98. Adore ye his footstool, because it is holy\+it*, and \+it Hebr. 11. He adored the top of his rod\+it*. ⁴ Thirdly, that the Scriptures also warrant us (as the nature of the word adoration giveth in all three tongues) to bow down our bodies, to fall flat on the ground at the presence of such things, and at the feet of holy persons, specially Angels, as John doth here, these examples prove. ⁵ Abraham adored the Angels that appeared to him, ⁶ Moyses also the Angel that shewed himself out of the bush, who were creatures, though they presented God's person, as this Angel here did, that spake to S. John. Balaam adored the Angel that stood before him with a sword drawn. Num 22. ⁷ Josue adored falling flat down before the feet of the Angel, ⁸ calling him his Lord, knowing by the Angel's own testimony, that it was but an Angel. Who refused it not, but required yet more reverence, commanding him to pluck off his shoes, because the ground was holy, no doubt so made by the presence only of the Angel. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Ps. 5, 137. Dan. 6. 3. Reg. 8. Ios. 7. Ps. 98. 131.\+it* \fq ² \ft 1 Religious worship of creatures. \fq ³ \ft 2 The same is called adoration. \fq ⁴ \ft 3 Falling prostrate before the persons or things adored. \fq ⁵ \ft \+it Gen. 18.\+it* \fq ⁶ \ft \+it Exod. 3.\+it* \fq ⁷ \ft Adoring of Angels. \fq ⁸ \ft \+it Iosue. 5.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 19.10 \ft Yea not only to Angels, ¹ but even to great Prophets this devotion was done, as to ² Daniel by Nabuchodonosor, who fell flat upon his face before him, and did other great offices of religion, which the Prophet refused not, because they were done to God rather than to him, as S. Hierom defendeth the same against Porphyrie, who charged Daniel with intolerable pride therein, and the said holy doctor allegeth the fact of Alexander the great, that did the like to ³ Joiadas the high priest of the Jews. Howsoever that be (for of the sacrifice there mentioned there may be some doubt, which the Church doth always immediately to God, and to no creature) the fact of the prophets (4 Reg. 2) to Elizeus, is plain: where they perceiving that the double grace and spirit of Elias was given to him, fell flat down at his feet and adored. So did ⁴ the Sunamite. To omit that Achior adored Judith, falling at her feet, as a woman blessed of God, and infinite other places. All which things, by comparing the Scriptures, our Adversaries should have found to be lawfully done to men, and Angels, and sovereign holy creatures. Whereby they might convince themselves, & perceive, that that thing could not be forbidden S. John to do to the Angel, which they pretend: though the Angel for causes might refuse even that which S. John did lawfully unto him, as S. Peter did refuse the honour given him by Cornelius, according to S. Chrysostom's opinion. \+it ho. 23 in c. 10 Act.\+it* yea even in the third chapter of this book (if our Adversaries would look no further) they might see where this Angel prophesieth & promiseth that the Jews should fall down before the feet of the Angel of Philadelphia and adore. See the Annot. there. \fq ¹ \ft Adoring of Prophets and holy persons. \fq ² \ft \+it Dan. 2.\+it* \fq ³ \ft \+it * or, Iaddus.\+it* \fq ⁴ \ft \+it 4. Reg. 4. Iudith. 13.\+it*\ef*
\v 11 And I saw heaven opened, and behold a white horse: and he that sat upon him, was called Faithful and True, and with justice he judgeth & fighteth.
\v 12 And his eyes as a flame of fire, and on his head many diadems, having a name written, which no man knoweth but himself.
\v 13 \x - \xt Esa. 63. 1.\x* And he was clothed with a garment sprinkled with blood: and his name is called, \f 2 \fr 19.13 \ft c The second person in Trinity, the Son or the Word of God, which was made flesh. \+it Io. 1.\+it*\f* \sc The Word of God\sc*.
\v 14 And the hosts that are in heaven followed him on white horses clothed in white and pure silk.
\v 15 And out of his mouth proceedeth a sharp sword: that in it he may strike the Gentiles. And \x - \xt Apoc. 2, 27.\x* he shall rule them in a rod of iron: and he treadeth the wine press of the fury of the wrath of God omnipotent.
\v 16 And he hath in his garment and in his thigh written, \x - \xt Apo. 17, 14.\x*\f 2 \fr 19.16 \ft Even according to his humanity also.\f* \sc King of Kings And Lord of Lords\sc*.
\v 17 And I saw one Angel standing in the sun, & he cried with a loud voice saying to all the birds that did fly by the midst of heaven: Come and assemble together to the great supper of God:
\v 18 that you may eat the flesh of kings, and the flesh of tribunes, and the flesh of valiants, and the flesh of horses and of them that sit on them, & the flesh of all free-men and bond-men, and of little and great.
\v 19 And I saw the beast and the kings of the earth, & their armies gathered to make war with him that sat upon the horse and with his army.
\v 20 And the beast was apprehended, and with him the false-prophet: which wrought signs before him, wherewith he seduced them that took the character of the beast, and that adored his image. These two were cast alive into the pool of fire burning also with brimstone.
\v 21 And the rest were slain by the sword of him that sitteth upon the horse, which proceedeth out of his mouth: and all the birds were filled with their flesh.
\c 20
\cd An Angel casteth the dragon (or devil) bound, into the depth for a thousand years, in which the souls of martyrs in the first resurrection shall reign with Christ. 7. After which years, Satan being let loose, shall raise Gog and Magog, an innumerable army, against the beloved city: 9. but a fire from heaven shall destroy them. 12. Then books are opened, and he that sitteth upon the throne, judgeth all the dead according to their works.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* \f 1 \fr 20.1 \ft See in S. Augustine \+it (li. 20. de Civit. c. 7. 8. & seq.)\+it* the exposition of this chapter.\f* I saw an Angel descending from heaven, having the key of the bottomless depth, and a great chain in his hand.
\v 2 And he apprehended the dragon the old serpent, which is the devil and Satan, and bound him for a thousand years. \ef - \fr 20.2 \fq Bound him. \ft Christ by his Passion hath abridged the power of the Devil for a thousand years, that is, the whole time of the new Testament, until Antichrist' time, when he shall be loosed again, that is, be permitted to deceive the world, but for a short time only, to wit, three years and a half.\ef*
\v 3 And he threw him into the depth, and shut him up, and sealed over him, that he seduce no more the nations, till the thousand years be consummate. And after these things he must be loosed a little time.
\v 4 And I saw seats: and they sat upon them, and judgement was given them, and the souls of the beheaded for the testimony of \sc Jesus\sc*, and for the word of God, and that adored not the beast, nor his image, nor received his character in their foreheads or in their hands, and have lived, & reigned with Christ \f 1 \fr 20.4 \ft \+it Quid in millenario numero nisi ad proferendam novam sobolem perfecta universitas præscitæ generationis exprimitur? hinc per Ioannem dicitur, Et regnabunt cum illo mille annis, quia regnum sanctæ Ecclesiæ, universitatis perfectione solidatur. D. Gregor. li. 9. Moral. c. 1.\+it*\f* a thousand years. \ef - \fr 20.4 \fq I saw seats. \ft S. Augustine \+it (li. 20 de Civit. Dei c. 9)\+it* taketh this to be spoken, \+it not of the last judgement, but of the Sees or Consistories of Bishops and Prelates,\+it* ¹ \+it and of the Prelates themselves, by whom the Church is now governed. As the judgement here given, can be taken no otherwise better, than of that which was said by our Saviour\+it* Mat. 18 \+it Whatsoever you bind in earth, shall be bound in heaven, and therefore the Apostle saith, What have I to do to judge of them that are without?\+it* ² \fq ¹ \ft Bishops' consistories & judicial power. \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Cor. 5.\+it*\ef* \ef - \fr 20.4 \fq And the souls. \ft \+it He meaneth\+it* (saith S. Augustine in the place alleged) \+it the souls of Martyrs, that they shall in the mean time, during these thousand years, which is the time of the Church militant, be in heaven without their bodies, and reign with Christ.\+it* ¹ \+it For the souls\+it* (saith he) \+it of the godly departed, are not separated from the Church, which is even now the kingdom of Christ. For else there should be kept no memory of them at the altar of God in the communicating of the body of Christ: neither should it avail to hasten to Baptism in the perils of death, for fear of ending our life without it: nor to hasten to be reconciled, if we fortune for penance or of ill conscience to be separated from the same body. And why are all these things done, but for that the faithful departed also be members of the Church? And though for an example the Martyrs be only named here, yet it is meant of others also that die in the state of grace.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft During a thousand years (that is the time of this militant Church) Saints reign with Christ in soul only.\ef*
\v 5 The rest of the dead lived not, till the thousand years be consummate. This is the first resurrection. \ef - \fr 20.5 \fq The rest lived not. \ft ¹ The rest which are not of the happy number aforesaid, but lived and died in sin, reign not with Christ in their souls during this time of the new Testament, but are dead in soul spiritually, and in body naturally, till the day of judgement. \+it S. August. ibidem.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The rest are dead and damned in soul, during the same time.\ef* \ef - \fr 20.5 \fq This is the first resurrection. \ft As there be two regenerations, one by faith, which is now in Baptism: and another according to the flesh, when at the later day the body shall be made immortal and incorruptible: so there are two resurrections, ¹ the one now of the souls to salvation when they die in grace, which is called the first, the other of the bodies at the later day. S. Augustine. \+it li. 20 de Civit. c. 6.\+it* \fq ¹ \ft The first resurrection, of the soul only.\ef*
\v 6 Blessed and holy is he that hath part in the first resurrection. In these the second death hath not power: but they shall be Priests of God and of Christ: and shall reign with him a thousand years. \ef - \fr 20.6 \fq They shall be Priests. \ft ¹ \+it It is not spoken\+it* (saith S. Augustine li. 20 de Civit. c. 10) \+it of Bishops and Priests only, which are properly now in the Church called Priests: but as we call all, Christians, for the mystical Chrism or ointment, so all, Priests, because they are the members of one Priest. Of whom the Apostle Peter saith, A holy people, a kingly Priesthood.\+it* ² Which words be notable for their learning that think there be none properly called Priests now in the new Testament, no otherwise then all Christian men and women, and a confusion to them that therefore have turned the name Priests into Ministers. \fq ¹ \ft Priests, some properly so called, some unproperly. \fq ² \ft \+it 1. Pet. 2.\+it*\ef*
\v 7 And when the thousand years shall be consummate, Satan shall be loosed out of his prison, and shall go forth and seduce \x - \xt Ezech. 38, 14. 39, 2.\x* the nations that are upon the four corners of the earth, \f 2 \fr 20.7 \ft S. Augustine thinketh that these do not signify any certain nations but all that shall then be joined with the Devil and Antichrist against the Church. \+it li. 20. de Civit. c. 11.\+it* See S. Hierom \+it in Ezech. li. 11.\+it*\f* Gog, and Magog, and shall gather them into battle, the number of whom is as the sand of the sea. \ef - \fr 20.7 \fq Satan shall be loosed. \ft In the whole 8 chapter of the said 20 book \+it de Civitate Dei\+it* in S. Augustine, is a notable commentary of these words. Where first he declareth, ¹ that neither this binding nor loosing of Satan is in respect of seducing or not seducing the Church of God: proving that whether he be bound or loose, he can never seduce the same. \+it The same,\+it* saith he, \+it shall be the state of the Church at that time when the Devil is to be loosed, even as since it was instituted. The same hath it been & shall be at all time in her children that succeed each other by birth & death.\+it* And a little after, \+it This I thought was therefore to be mentioned, lest any man should think, that during the little time wherein the Devil shall be loosed, the Church shall not be upon the earth, he either not finding it here when he shall be let loose, or consuming it when he shall by all means persecute the same.\+it* Secondly he declareth, that the Devil to be bound, is nothing else but not to be permitted by God to exercise all his force or fraud in tentations: as to be loosed, is to be suffered by God for a small time, that is, for three years and a half, to practise and prove all his power and arts of tentations against the Church and her children, and yet not to prevail against them. Thirdly this Doctor sheweth by what great mercy our Lord hath tied Satan and abridged his power during the whole millenary or thousand years, which is all the time of the new Testament until then: & with what wisdom he permitteth him to break loose that little time of three years and six months, ² toward the later day, which shall be the reign of Antichrist. Lastly he sheweth what kind of men shall be most subject to the Devil's seduction, (even such as now by tentation of Heretics go out of the Church) and who shall avoid it. \fq ¹ \ft The binding and loosing of Satan, explicated by S. Augustine. \fq ² \ft The short reign of Antichrist.\ef* \ef - \fr 20.7 \ft By all which we may confute diverse false expositions of old & late Heretics. First, the ancient sect of the Millenaries, ¹ that grounded upon these thousand years named by the Prophet, this heresy, that there should be so many years after the resurrection of our bodies, in which we should reign with Christ in this world, in our bodies, in all delights and pleasures corporal, of meats, drinks, and such like, which they called the first resurrection, of which heresy Cerinthus was the author. \+it Epiph. hær. 77 in fine. Hiero. Comment. in c. 19 Matt. August. hær. 8 ad Quod vult Deum.\+it* Eusebius also \+it (li. 3 historia c. 33)\+it* sheweth that some principal men were in part (though after a more honest manner concerning those corporal delicacies) of the same opinion, by misconstruction of these words of S. John. Whereby we learn and all the world may perceive, the holy Scriptures to be hard, ² when so great clerks did err, and that there is no security but in that sense which the Church alloweth of. \fq ¹ \ft \+it Millenarii\+it* or \+it Chiliastæ\+it*. \fq ² \ft The Scriptures hard.\ef* \ef - \fr 20.7 \ft ¹ The late Heretics also by the said S. Augustine's words are fully refuted, affirming not only that the Church may be seduced in that great persecution of the Devil's loosing, but that it hath been seduced even a great piece of this time when the Devil is bound: holding that the very true Church may err or fall from truth to error and idolatry, yea (which is more blasphemy) that the chief governor of the Church is Antichrist himself, and the very Church under him, the whore of Babylon: and that this Antichrist, (which the Scriptures in so many places, and here plainly by S. Augustine's exposition, testify, shall reign but a small time, and that toward the last judgement,) hath been revealed long sithence, to be the Pope himself, Christ's own Vicar, and that he hath persecuted the Saints of their sect for these thousand years at the least. Which is no more but to make the Devil to be loose, and Antichrist to reign the whole thousand years, or the most part thereof, that is, almost the whole time of the Church's state in the new Testament: (which is against this and other Scriptures evidently, appointing that, to be the time of the Devil's binding:) ² Yea it is to make Antichrist and the Devil weaker toward the day of judgement than before, and the truth better to be known, and the faith more common, the nearer we come to the same judgement: which is expressly against the Gospel, and this prophecy of S. John. ³ We see that the sects of Luther, Calvin, and other, be more spread through the world than they were ever before, and consequently the Pope and his religion lessened, and his power of punishing (or, as they call it) persecuting the said Sectaries, through the multitude of his adversaries, diminished. How then is the Pope Antichrist, whose force shall be greater at the later end of the world, than before? Or how can it be otherwise, but these sect-Masters should be Antichrist's near precursors, that make Christ's chief Ministers & the Church's chief governors that have been these thousand years and more, to be Antichrists: and themselves and their sects to be true, that come so near the time of the Devil's loosing and seduction & of the personal reign of Antichrist? \fq ¹ \ft By S. Augustine's foresaid explication, is evidently deduced against the Protestants, that the Church cannot err, and that the Pope cannot possibly be Antichrist. \fq ² \ft An invincible demonstration. \fq ³ \ft \+it Mat. 24. Lu. 18, 8.\+it*\ef*
\v 8 And they ascended upon the breadth of the earth, and compassed the camp of the Saints, and the beloved city. \ef - \fr 20.8 \fq The camp of the Saints. \ft ¹ S. Augustine in the said 20 book de Civit. Dei cap. 11. \+it It is not\+it*, saith he, \+it to be taken that the persecutors shall gather to any place, as though the camp of the saints or the beloved city should be but in one place, which indeed are no other thing but the Church of Christ spread through the whole world. And therefore wheresoever the Church shall then be (which shall be in all Nations even then, for so much is insinuated by this latitude of the earth here specified) there shall the tents of saints be, and the beloved city of God, and there shall she be besieged by all her enemies, which shall be in every country where she is, in most cruel and forcible sort.\+it* So writeth this profound holy Doctor. Whereby we see, that, ² as now the particular Churches of England, Scotland, Flanders, and such like, be persecuted by their enemies in those countries, so in the time of Antichrist, the Churches of all Nations, as of Italy, Spain, France, and all other which now be quiet, shall be assaulted as now the foresaid are, and much more, for that the general persecution of the whole, shall be greater than the particular persecution of any Churches in the world. \fq ¹ \ft The camp of Saints is the Catho. Church through the world. \fq ² \ft As now Heretics in particular countries, so Antichrist shall persecute the Churches of all nations.\ef*
\v 9 And there came down fire from God out of heaven, and devoured them: \ef - \fr 20.9 \fq There came down a fire. \ft ¹ It is not meant of the fire of Hell \+it (saith S. Augustine ib. c. 12)\+it* into which the wicked shall be cast after the resurrection of their bodies, but of an extraordinary help that God will send from heaven, to give succourse to the Saints of the Church that then shall fight against the wicked: or, the very fervent and burning zeal of religion and God's honour, which God will kindle in the hearts of the faithful, to be constant against all the forces of that great persecution. \fq ¹ \ft What is meant by fire from heaven.\ef*
\v 10 and the devil which seduced them, was cast into the pool of fire and brimstone, where both the beast & the false-prophet shall be tormented day and night for ever and ever.
\v 11 \f 1 \fr 20.11 \ft THE 6. VISION.\f* And I saw a great white throne, and one sitting upon it, from whose sight \f 2 \fr 20.11 \ft They shall then be new, not the substance, but the shape changed. \+it 2. Pet. 3.\+it* See S. Augustine. \+it li. 20. de Civit. c. 14.\+it*\f* earth and heaven fled, and there was no place found for them.
\v 12 And I saw the dead, great and little, standing in the sight of the throne, and \x - \xt Apoc. 3, 5. 21, 27.\x* books were opened: and \f 2 \fr 20.12 \ft c The books of men's consciences, where it shall plainly be read what every man's life hath been.\f* another book was opened, which is of life: and the dead were judged of those things which were written in the books according to their works. \ef - \fr 20.12 \fq Another book. \ft ¹ This is the book of God's knowledge or predestination, wherein that which before was hid to the world, shall be opened, and wherein the true record of every man's works shall be contained, and they have their judgement diversly according to their works, and not according to faith only, or lack of faith only. For, all infidels (as Turks, obstinate Jews, and Heretics) shall never come to that examination, being otherwise condemned. \fq ¹ \ft The book of every man's works, opened in the day of judgement.\ef*
\v 13 And the sea gave the dead that were in it, and death and hell gave their dead that were in them, and it was judged of every one according to their works.
\v 14 And hell and death were cast into the pool of fire. This is the second death.
\v 15 And \x - \xt Mat. 24. Lu. 18, 8.\x* he that was not found written in the book of life, was cast into the pool of fire.
\c 21
\cd Heaven and earth being made new, S. John seeth the new city Hierusalem prepared and adorned for the spouse of the Lamb. 6. The just are glorified, 7. and the wicked thrust into the pool of fire. 12. The wall and gates and foundations of the city are described and measured: 18. all which are gold and crystal, precious stones and pearls. \f - \ft THE 5. PART.\f* \f - \ft The final glorification of the Church.\f*
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* I saw a new heaven and a new earth. For \x - \xt Esa. 63, 17. 66, 21. 2. Pet. 3, 13.\x* the first heaven, and the first earth was gone, & the sea now is not.
\v 2 \f 1 \fr 21.2 \ft The Epistle upon the dedication of a Church.\f* And I John saw \f 2 \fr 21.2 \ft c The Church triumphant.\f* the holy city Jerusalem new descending from heaven, prepared of God, as a bride adorned for her husband.
\v 3 And I heard a loud voice from the throne saying: Behold \f 1 \fr 21.3 \ft This tabernacle is Christ according to his humanity.\f* the tabernacle of God with men, and he will dwell with them. And they shall be his people: and he God with them shall be their God.
\v 4 And \x - \xt Esa. 25. 8. Apoc. 7, 17.\x* God shall \f 2 \fr 21.4 \ft c This happy day shall make an end of all the miseries of this mortality.\f* wipe away all tears from their eyes: and death shall be no more, nor mourning, nor crying, neither shall there be sorrow any more, \f 3 \fr 21.4 \fq which \ft \+it because the\+it*\f* which first things, are gone.
\v 5 And he that sat in the throne, said: \x - \xt Esa. 43, 19.\x* Behold I make all things new. And he said to me: Write, because these words be most faithful and true.
\v 6 And he said to me: It is done, \x - \xt Apoc. 1, 8. 22, 13.\x* I am Alpha and Omega: the beginning and the end. To him that thirsteth I will give of the fountain of the water of life, gratis.
\v 7 He that shall overcome, \f 1 \fr 21.7 \ft He that hath the victory against sin in the Church militant, shall have his reward in the triumphant.\f* shall possess these things, and I will be his God: and he shall be my son.
\v 8 But \f 1 \fr 21.8 \ft c All that commit mortal sins and repent not, shall be damned.\f* to the fearful, and incredulous, and execrable, and murderers, and fornicators, and sorcerers, and idolaters, and all liars, their part shall be in the pool burning with fire and brimstone, which is the second death.
\v 9 \f 1 \fr 21.9 \ft THE 7. AND LAST VISION.\f* And there came one of the seven Angels that had the vials full of the seven last plagues, and spake with me, saying: Come, and I will shew thee the bride, the wife of the Lamb.
\v 10 And he took me up in spirit unto a mountain great and high: and he shewed me the holy city Jerusalem descending out of heaven from God,
\v 11 \f 1 \fr 21.11 \ft The glory of the Church triumphant.\f* having the glory of God, and the light thereof like to a precious stone, as it were to the jasper stone, even as crystal.
\v 12 And it had a wall great and high, having twelve gates, and in the gates twelve Angels, & names written thereon, which are \f 1 \fr 21.12 \ft c The names of the Patriarchs and Apostles honourable and glorious in the triumphant Church.\f* the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Israel.
\v 13 On the East, three gates: and on the North, three gates: and on the South, three gates: and on the West, three gates.
\v 14 And the wall of the city having twelve foundations: and in them, twelve names, of the twelve Apostles of the Lamb.
\v 15 And he that spake with me, had a measure of a reed, of gold, to measure the city and the gates thereof, and the wall.
\v 16 And \f 1 \fr 21.16 \ft See S. Hierom \+it ep. 17.\+it* touching this description of the heavenly Hierusalem, which is the Church triumphant, teaching that these things must be taken spiritually, not carnally.\f* the city is situated quadrangle-wise, and the length thereof is as great as also the breadth: and he measured the city with the reed for twelve thousand furlongs, & the length and height and breadth thereof be equal.
\v 17 And he measured the wall thereof of an hundred forty four cubits, the measure of a man which is of an Angel.
\v 18 And the building of the wall thereof was of jasper stone: but the city it-self pure gold, like to pure glass. \ef - \fr 21.18 \fq Pure gold. \ft S. Gregory \+it (li. 18. Moral. c. 23)\+it* saith, the heavenly state is resembled to gold, precious stone, crystal, glass, and the like, for the purity, clarity, glittering of the glorious bodies: ¹ where one man's body, conscience, and cogitations are represented to another, as corporal things in this life are seen through crystal or glass. \fq ¹ \ft The state of glorified bodies.\ef*
\v 19 And the foundations of the wall of the city, were adorned with all precious stone. The first foundation, the jasper: the second, the sapphire: the third, the calcedonius: the fourth, the emerald:
\v 20 the fifth, the sardonix: the sixth, the sardius: the seventh, the chrysolithus: the eight, the beryllus: the ninth, the topazius: the tenth, the chrysoprasus: the eleventh, the hyacinth: the twelfth, the amethyst.
\v 21 And the twelve gates: there are twelve pearls, one to every one: & every gate was of one several pearl. And the street of the city pure gold, as it were transparent glass.
\v 22 And \f 1 \fr 21.22 \ft All external sacrifice which now is necessary duty of the faithful, shall then cease and therefore there shall need no material temple.\f* temple I saw not therein. For our Lord the God omnipotent is the temple thereof, and the Lamb.
\v 23 And \x - \xt Esa. 60, 19.\x* the city needeth not sun nor moon, to shine in it. For the glory of God hath illuminated it, and the Lamb is the lamp thereof.
\v 24 And \x - \xt Esa. 60, 3.\x* the Gentiles shall walk in the light of it: and the kings of the earth shall bring their glory and honour into it.
\v 25 And \x - \xt Esa. 60, 11.\x* the gates thereof shall not be shut by day: for there shall be no night there.
\v 26 And they shall bring the glory and honour of nations into it.
\v 27 There shall \x - \xt Apo. 20, 12.\x* not enter into it any polluted thing, nor that doeth abomination and maketh lie, but \f 2 \fr 21.27 \ft None not perfectly cleansed of their sins, can enter into this heavenly Hierusalem.\f* they that are written in the book of life of the Lamb.
\c 22
\cd The tree of life being watered with living water, yieldeth fruits every month. 3. There is neither curse nor night in the city. 6. The Angel that shewed John all these things, refuseth to be adored of him 14. He telleth him that the just shall enter into the city, but the rest shall be cast forth. 18. Lastly, he protesteth and threateneth against them that shall presume to add to this prophecy, or take away from the same.
\p
\v 1 \sc And\sc* he shewed me a river of \f 1 \fr 22.1 \fq living water \ft \+it water of life,\+it*\f* living water, clear as crystal, proceeding from the seat of God and of the Lamb.
\v 2 In the midst of the street thereof, & on both sides of the river, \f 1 \fr 22.2 \ft Christ is our tree of life; in the Church, by the B. Sacrament; & in heaven, by his visible presence and influence of life everlasting both to our bodies & souls: of whom Salomon saith, \+it The tree of life to all that apprehend him. Prov. 3.\+it*\f* the tree of life, yielding twelve fruits, rendering his fruit every month, & the leaves of the tree for the curing of the Gentiles.
\v 3 And no curse shall be any more: & the seat of God & of the Lamb shall be in it, & his servants shall serve him.
\v 4 And they shall see his face: and his name in their foreheads.
\v 5 And \x - \xt Apo. 21. Esa. 60.\x* night shall be no more: and they shall not need the light of lamp, nor the light of the sun, because our Lord God doth illuminate them, and they shall reign for ever and ever.
\v 6 \f 1 \fr 22.6 \ft THE CONCLUSION.\f* And he said to me: These words are most faithful & true. And our Lord the God of the spirits of the Prophets, sent his Angel to shew his servants those things which must be done quickly.
\v 7 And behold I come quickly. Blessed is he that keepeth the words of the prophecy of this book.
\v 8 And I John which have heard, and seen these things. And \x - \xt Apo. 19, 10.\x* after I had heard and seen, I fell down \f 2 \fr 22.8 \ft You see it is all one to adore before the feet of the Angel, & to adore the Angel: though, to adore him, be not expressed as in the 19. chap. \+it See the annotation there v. 10.\+it*\f* to adore before the feet of the Angel which shewed me these things;
\v 9 and he said to me: See thou do not, for I am thy fellow-servant, and of thy brethren the Prophets, and of them that keep the words of this book. Adore God.
\v 10 And he saith to me: Seal not the words of the prophecy of this book. For the time is near.
\v 11 He that hurteth, let him hurt yet: and he that is in filth, let him be filthy yet: and he that is just, \f 1 \fr 22.11 \ft c Man by God's grace & doing good works, doth increase his justice.\f* let him be justified yet: and let the holy be sanctified yet. \ef - \fr 22.11 \fq He that hurteth. \ft It is not an exhortation, but a commination or threatening, that how far soever the wicked increase in naughtiness, God hath provided answerable punishment for them.\ef*
\v 12 Behold I come quickly. And my reward is with me, \x - \xt Ro. 2, 6.\x* to render to every man \f 2 \fr 22.12 \ft Heaven is the reward, hire, & repayment for good works, in all the Scriptures, yet the adversaries will not see it.\f* according to his works.
\v 13 I am \x - \xt Apo. 21, 6. 1, 8.\x* Alpha and Omega, the first and the last, the beginning and the end.
\v 14 Blessed are they that wash their stoles: that their power may be in the tree of life, and they may enter by the gates into the city.
\v 15 Without are dogs and sorcerers, and the unchaste, and murderers, and servers of idols, & every one that loveth and maketh a lie.
\v 16 I \sc Jesus\sc* have sent mine Angel, to testify to you these things in the Churches. I am the root and stock of David, the bright and morning star.
\v 17 And the Spirit & the bride say, Come. And he that heareth, let him say, Come. And \x - \xt Esa. 55, 1.\x* he that thirsteth, let him come: and he that will, let him take the water of life gratis.
\v 18 For I testify to every one hearing the words of the prophecy of this book, If any man shall add to these things, God shall add upon him the plagues written in this book. \ef - \fr 22.18 \fq If any man shall add. \ft ¹ The author of the commentaries upon this book, bearing the name of S. Ambrose, saith thus of this point. \+it He maketh not this protestation against the expositor of his prophecy, but against Heretics. For the expositor doth add or diminish nothing, but openeth the obscurity of the narration, or sheweth the moral or spiritual sense. He curseth therefore Heretics, that used to add somewhat of their own that was false, and to take away other things that were contrary to their heresies.\+it* So saith this ancient writer. And this was the property of them in all ages, and so is it of ours now, as we have noted through the whole Bible, and as we have in sundry places set forth to the sight of all indifferent readers, in the new Testament, that all the world may see that the Apostle's curse is fallen upon them and may beware of them. \fq ¹ \ft The curse against adding or diminishing, is against Heretics, not Catholic expositors.\ef*
\v 19 And if any man shall diminish of the words of the book of this prophecy: God shall take away his part out of the book of life, and out of the holy city, and of these things that be written in this book,
\v 20 saith he that giveth testimony of these things. Yea I come quickly: Amen. Come Lord \sc Jesus\sc*. \ef - \fr 22.20 \fq Come Lord Jesus. \ft ¹ And now o Lord Christ, most just and merciful, we thy poor creatures that are so afflicted for confession and defence of the holy, Catholic, and Apostolic truth, contained in this thy sacred book, and in the infallible doctrine of thy dear spouse our mother the Church, we cry also unto thy Majesty with tenderness of our hearts unspeakable: \+sc Come Lord Jesus Quickly\+sc*, and judge betwixt us and our Adversaries, and in the mean time give patience, comfort, and constancy to all that suffer for thy name, and trust in thee. O Lord God our only helper and \fq ¹ \ft A brief petition unto JESUS Christ, to come quickly, as S. John here speaketh, and to judge the cause of Catholics & Protestants.\ef*
\v 21 The grace of our Lord \sc Jesus\sc* Christ be with you all. Amen.
